window.ISTLAB = {"lessons":{"1.1":{"id":"1.1","title":"The CIA Triad","back":"../unit-1/index.html","w":{"pts":50,"intro":"You will go through the 1.1 notes one part at a time. Each step tells you which part to open, then asks questions that only someone who read that part can answer. You need 100% to open the assignment, and you can check as many times as you like.","steps":[{"h":"The three questions","read":"<p>Open the notes and read the top of the page, from the title down to the three cards: <b>Confidentiality</b>, <b>Integrity</b> and <b>Availability</b>. Each card asks one short question and gives an analogy.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the 1.1 notes","href":"../unit-1/1-1-notes.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q1","type":"match","q":"Match each pillar to the analogy the notes use for it.","left":["Confidentiality","Integrity","Availability"],"right":["The power and water at your house","A safety seal on a food container","A sealed letter or a diary with a lock"]},{"id":"q2","type":"mc","q":"Which of the three short questions on the cards goes with <b>Integrity</b>?","opts":["Is it accurate?","Is it free to use?","Is it there when I need it?","Is it secret?"]},{"id":"q3","type":"multi","q":"The first paragraph says you already practice cybersecurity every day. Which three everyday things does it name?","opts":["Changing your ringtone","Charging your phone overnight","Getting annoyed when Wi-Fi drops","Locking your phone","Checking your bank balance"]}]},{"h":"Part 01: Confidentiality","read":"<p>Scroll to <b>01: C, Confidentiality</b>. Read all ten examples and the red <b>When it fails</b> paragraph under them.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the 1.1 notes","href":"../unit-1/1-1-notes.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q4","type":"mc","q":"Which example is described as \"confidentiality with a built-in expiration date\"?","opts":["Two-factor authentication","A disappearing Snapchat photo","Incognito browsing","A private Instagram account"]},{"id":"q5","type":"mc","q":"The notes call one thing \"the single fastest way confidentiality actually breaks for a teenager.\" What is it?","opts":["A site that loses its security certificate overnight","A weak Wi-Fi signal in the school building","A phone left unlocked, such as lending it to a friend","A hacker in another country guessing your password"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mc","q":"According to the notes, who does Incognito or private browsing mode hide your search history from?","opts":["Your internet provider and its servers","Every website you visit during the session","Your school and everything on its network","Anyone else who uses the same shared device"]},{"id":"q7","type":"mc","q":"What does two-factor authentication (2FA) stop, according to the notes?","opts":["Someone logging in with only your stolen password","Your phone from running out of battery power","A shared file from being edited by a teammate","Anyone from ever seeing your username online"]}]},{"h":"Part 02: Integrity","read":"<p>Scroll to <b>02: I, Integrity</b>. Read all ten examples and the <b>When it fails</b> paragraph. This is the pillar the notes say most people forget about.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the 1.1 notes","href":"../unit-1/1-1-notes.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q8","type":"mc","q":"Why does Google Docs <b>version history</b> matter on a group project, according to the notes?","opts":["It makes a long document load faster on slow Wi-Fi","It can catch someone quietly rewriting a teammate's paragraph","It keeps the document online during an outage","It keeps your document secret from every outsider"]},{"id":"q9","type":"mc","q":"A friend sends a screenshot as \"proof\" you said something. The notes say the image itself can be a problem for which pillar?","opts":["None of the pillars, since a screenshot is only a picture","Availability, since a large image is slow to open","Integrity, since screenshots can be edited before sending","Confidentiality, since the screenshot shows a private chat"]},{"id":"q10","type":"multi","q":"Which of these does the notes' Integrity list include as examples of data being changed or faked? Pick all that apply.","opts":["School Wi-Fi going down before a deadline","A deepfaked video or Photoshopped photo","A text thread forwarded without the rest of the conversation","A phone left unlocked at lunch","Autocorrect changing what you typed"]}]},{"h":"Part 03: Availability","read":"<p>Scroll to <b>03: A, Availability</b>. Read all ten examples and the <b>When it fails</b> paragraph.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the 1.1 notes","href":"../unit-1/1-1-notes.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q11","type":"mc","q":"The notes say a system can be \"perfectly private and perfectly accurate\" and still be useless. When?","opts":["When it is stored in only one language","When its password is much too long to type","When it has more users than expected","When it is down and you cannot reach it"]},{"id":"q12","type":"mc","q":"Why does the notes list ransomware locking a school's computers under <b>Availability</b>?","opts":["The files stay, but nobody can open them without paying","The school forgot the password to its own Wi-Fi network","The attackers quietly changed the grades in the portal","The attackers read every file on the system in secret"]},{"id":"q13","type":"mc","q":"During a DDoS attack, the notes say the website is \"up\" but what?","opts":["Turned into a private site that needs a login","Unreachable, because fake traffic crowds out real visitors","Showing visitors information that was changed by someone","Secretly copied onto the attacker's own server"]}]},{"h":"Part 04: Putting it together","read":"<p>Scroll to <b>04: Putting It Together</b>. Read the table and the paragraph about the three pillars pulling against each other.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the 1.1 notes","href":"../unit-1/1-1-notes.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q14","type":"mc","q":"The notes use a hospital as an example of a tradeoff. What was traded away?","opts":["Nothing, because the setup was perfect","Integrity, to get more availability","Availability, to get more confidentiality","Confidentiality, to get more integrity"]},{"id":"q15","type":"num","q":"How many examples does the notes list for each pillar?"},{"id":"q16","type":"mc","q":"What does the notes say good security is about?","opts":["Protecting confidentiality above everything else","Using as many different passwords as possible","Getting a perfect score on every single pillar","Choosing the right balance for the situation"]},{"id":"q17","type":"write","q":"In your own words, explain why pushing one pillar too hard can cost another. Use the hospital example from the notes. (2 to 3 sentences)","min":25}]}]},"a":{"pts":100,"company":"Spotify","color":"#1DB954","tagline":"You are a summer security analyst on Spotify's trust team. Your manager sends you the week's incident reports.","from":"Maya Brooks, Security Team Lead","subject":"Monday triage: six incidents to label by pillar","email":"<p>Welcome to the team. Every incident that comes in gets labeled with the pillar of the CIA Triad that failed, so the right engineers see it first.</p><p>Below are this week's reports and a few decisions I need from you. Work through them in order. This is a classroom simulation, so the details are made up.</p>","steps":[{"h":"Label the incidents","read":"<p>Match each incident report to the pillar that failed.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q1","type":"match","q":"Match each incident to the pillar that failed.","left":["A hacker reads the private playlists and listening history of 40,000 users.","Someone edits the play counts on a song, so the artist is paid the wrong amount.","A flood of fake traffic makes the app unreachable on release day."],"right":["Integrity","Availability","Confidentiality"]},{"id":"q2","type":"match","q":"Now three more.","left":["Ransomware locks the internal tools the support team needs.","An employee emails a customer list to the wrong person.","A fake \"verified\" artist account posts songs under a real artist's name."],"right":["Confidentiality","Integrity","Availability"]}]},{"h":"Decide the fix","read":"<p>Pick the best response to each problem. Think about which pillar is at risk.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q3","type":"mc","q":"Royalty numbers keep getting changed by accident when two people edit the same spreadsheet. Which tool protects the <b>integrity</b> of that data?","opts":["Version history that shows who changed what","A second internet provider for the building","A private setting on every employee account","A longer Wi-Fi password in the office"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mc","q":"Support agents need to read customer account details quickly during a live outage. Which change would protect <b>availability</b>?","opts":["Turn off two-factor authentication for everyone","Keep working backups and a second way into the tools","Delete the oldest customer accounts to save space","Hide all customer data from every employee at once"]},{"id":"q5","type":"mc","q":"A manager wants a new rule: every change to payment data needs three approvals. What is the likely tradeoff?","opts":["Less confidentiality and less integrity for the data","No tradeoff, because extra approvals never cost anything","More protection, but slower access when it is urgent","More availability and a lot less integrity overall"]}]},{"h":"Write back to your lead","read":"<p>Maya wants a short note she can forward. Write two to three sentences that cover each part below.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q6","type":"write","q":"Pick one incident from the first task. Name the pillar that failed, say what it means in plain words, and give one thing Spotify could do to prevent it.","min":30}]}]}},"1.2":{"id":"1.2","title":"OSINT: Open Source Intelligence","back":"../unit-1/index.html","w":{"pts":50,"intro":"You will go through the OSINT guide one part at a time. Every step names the part to open, then asks questions that only someone who read it can answer. You need 100% to open the assignment, and you can check as many times as you like.","steps":[{"h":"Part 00: What is OSINT?","read":"<p>Open the guide and read <b>00: What Is OSINT?</b> from the first paragraph to the end of the section, including \"The definition\" and \"What open source means.\"</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the 1.2 OSINT guide","href":"../unit-1/1-2-osint-guide.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q1","type":"mc","q":"What does OSINT stand for?","opts":["Online Security Intelligence","Open Systems Investigation","Official Source Information","Open Source Intelligence"]},{"id":"q2","type":"mc","q":"What does \"open source\" mean in this guide?","opts":["Available to anyone who knows where to look","Hacked by a person with special tools","Leaked from a company's private servers","Free to copy without any credit at all"]},{"id":"q3","type":"mc","q":"What do hackers almost always do before they try to break into a system?","opts":["Buy a new computer and a new account","OSINT, to learn about their target first","Delete the logs from the target system","Ask the target for permission to test it"]}]},{"h":"Part 01: The five main sources","read":"<p>Read <b>01: The Five Main Sources</b>. Notice the example sites named in each box.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the 1.2 OSINT guide","href":"../unit-1/1-2-osint-guide.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q4","type":"match","q":"Match each source to the example the guide gives for it.","left":["Search engines and the open web","Public records and government databases","News, blogs and archives","Data aggregators and people-search sites"],"right":["Old pages saved on the Wayback Machine","Court records, property deeds and voter rolls","Shodan, which searches internet-connected devices","FastPeopleSearch, Spokeo and BeenVerified"]},{"id":"q5","type":"mc","q":"According to \"The combined effect,\" how long can it take a stranger to build a frighteningly complete picture of you?","opts":["A full month of work using paid tools only","About a week of careful, patient research work","Under an hour, without hacking anything","It cannot be done without a large data breach"]}]},{"h":"Part 02: Who uses OSINT?","read":"<p>Read <b>02: Who Uses OSINT?</b>. It sorts people into three groups: the good guys, everyday users, and the concerning side.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the 1.2 OSINT guide","href":"../unit-1/1-2-osint-guide.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q6","type":"match","q":"Match each user to what the guide says they do with OSINT.","left":["Journalists","Cybersecurity professionals","Scammers","Stalkers and doxxers"],"right":["Learn names and schedules to write a fake email that looks real","Verify stories and uncover things people in power hide","Find what an attacker would see first, then fix it","Find a home address, school and family"]},{"id":"q7","type":"text","q":"What is it called when a scammer uses OSINT to write a fake email that looks shockingly real to one specific person?","ph":"Two words"},{"id":"q8","type":"mc","q":"The guide says OSINT is like a kitchen knife. What changes between a journalist and a stalker?","opts":["The information, because the sources differ","The law, because stalking is always legal","The tools, because only experts own them","The intent, because the information is identical"]}]},{"h":"Part 03: Five public posts","read":"<p>Read <b>03: A Real Example</b> about Jordan's five public Instagram posts. For each post, notice what a stranger learns.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the 1.2 OSINT guide","href":"../unit-1/1-2-osint-guide.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q9","type":"mc","q":"Why does the guide say a dog's name in a photo caption matters?","opts":["Dog names are often used in passwords","The dog's name proves who owns the account","Dogs have to be registered with the police","Strangers can then see the dog's home address"]},{"id":"q10","type":"mc","q":"Why does a \"happy birthday Mom!\" post give away useful information?","opts":["It shows exactly when the whole family is on vacation","Mom's name and birthday are common security questions","Instagram sells the birthday to the school district","Birthdays are always listed in public court records"]},{"id":"q11","type":"text","q":"The hidden data inside a photo file that can include the exact GPS coordinates has a four-letter name. What is it?","ph":"Four letters"},{"id":"q12","type":"num","q":"The guide says that with zero hacking a stranger learns all of this in under how many minutes?"}]},{"h":"Part 04: OSINT and the CIA Triad","read":"<p>Read <b>04: OSINT and the CIA Triad</b>. Each pillar has a real example.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the 1.2 OSINT guide","href":"../unit-1/1-2-osint-guide.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q13","type":"match","q":"Match each pillar to the real example the guide gives for how OSINT strains it.","left":["Confidentiality","Integrity","Availability"],"right":["You delete a tweet but it was already screenshotted and archived","A typo in a public arrest record names the wrong person","A private thought you posted at 14 is still Googleable at 25"]},{"id":"q14","type":"mc","q":"In Part 04, why is availability a problem for privacy?","opts":["Only the owner can ever reach the data again later","Sites go offline so often that nobody can rely on them","Public data is too available: it is copied everywhere","Public data is never available when you need it most"]}]},{"h":"Part 05: Registries, blotters and aggregators","read":"<p>Read <b>05: Case Study: Registries and Blotters</b>, including the three \"Where Each Pillar Breaks\" boxes and the table.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the 1.2 OSINT guide","href":"../unit-1/1-2-osint-guide.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q15","type":"mc","q":"What is a police blotter, according to the guide?","opts":["A tool that quietly deletes old arrest records for you","A national list of every person ever convicted of a crime","A private file that is kept only by police detectives","A daily news feed of arrests and calls for service"]},{"id":"q16","type":"multi","q":"Which are the three ways data aggregators get your data?","opts":["Buying and selling data","Public records","Reading your private texts","Guessing your password","Social media scraping"]},{"id":"q17","type":"mc","q":"The guide says a typo on a blotter lists the wrong name for an arrest. Which pillar failed?","opts":["Integrity, because the data must be true to be fair","None, because the data was public anyway","Availability, because the blotter went offline","Confidentiality, because the arrest was secret"]},{"id":"q18","type":"mc","q":"Why might someone proven innocent still find their mugshot hard to remove?","opts":["Mugshots are protected by strict copyright law","Some sites charge a fee, and archives keep copies","Search engines do not accept any removal requests","Courts are not allowed to delete any record at all"]}]},{"h":"Part 07: Defend yourself","read":"<p>Read <b>07: Defend Yourself</b> and the closing paragraph about hygiene.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the 1.2 OSINT guide","href":"../unit-1/1-2-osint-guide.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q19","type":"mc","q":"What is the goal of OSINT hygiene, according to the guide?","opts":["Deleting every account that you have ever made","Hiding from every single person who might search you","Making sure what is out there is what you chose","Becoming completely invisible on the whole internet"]},{"id":"q20","type":"write","q":"In your own words, explain how five posts that each look harmless can still be risky. Use Jordan as your example. (2 to 3 sentences)","min":25}]}]},"a":{"pts":100,"company":"Zillow","color":"#006AFF","tagline":"You are a trust and safety intern. Your team checks what a stranger could learn from public information before a new feature launches.","from":"Dana Ortiz, Trust and Safety Manager","subject":"Review a sample profile before we launch the new seller page","email":"<p>Hi, we are adding a seller page that shows an agent's name, listings and a short bio. Before launch I want a safety review.</p><p>Use what you know about OSINT. Our test person is Riley, a 17-year-old who volunteers for a family friend who sells homes. All of Riley's posts are public. Work through the tasks in order.</p>","steps":[{"h":"What does a stranger learn?","read":"<p>Riley posted: (1) a photo in a school hoodie at a Friday football game, (2) a selfie in the driveway with the house number visible, (3) \"Mom's birthday dinner on the 14th!\", (4) \"Heading to the lake all weekend with the whole family,\" (5) a story saying \"AP test Thursday 2nd period.\"</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q1","type":"match","q":"Match each post to the main thing a stranger learns from it.","left":["The school hoodie at the football game","The driveway selfie with the house number","Mom's birthday dinner on the 14th","Heading to the lake all weekend","AP test Thursday 2nd period"],"right":["Riley's school and a day the team plays","Where Riley will be at what time","The house will be empty","Where Riley lives","A birthday and a family name"]},{"id":"q2","type":"mc","q":"Post 4 (the weekend at the lake) is the riskiest of the five. Why?","opts":["It reveals Riley's full legal name to strangers","It shows Riley's face to the entire public","It names a lake somewhere in the state of Georgia","It tells everyone that nobody is home for a while"]}]},{"h":"Where the information comes from","read":"<p>Sort the sources a researcher would use to build a profile of an agent or a seller.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q3","type":"match","q":"Match each source type to something Zillow's reviewers might find there.","left":["Public records","Social media","Data aggregators","News and archives"],"right":["A property deed with the owner's name","An old school newsletter on the Wayback Machine","Photos with a street sign in the background","One profile that combines address, relatives and phone"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mc","q":"A reviewer finds that a seller's home address, age and relatives all appear on a people-search site. Which sentence is the most accurate?","opts":["None of it was hacked; it was gathered from public sources","The site must have broken into private databases","It is illegal for anyone to gather this data","This is only possible for famous people"]}]},{"h":"Pillars and recommendations","read":"<p>Dana wants the team to name the pillar involved and suggest a fix.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q5","type":"mc","q":"A seller's page shows the wrong phone number because a scraper copied an old listing. Which pillar is the problem?","opts":["Confidentiality, because the phone number was private","Integrity, because the information is not accurate","None, because a phone number is already public","Availability, because the page loaded far too slowly"]},{"id":"q6","type":"multi","q":"Which changes would reduce what a stranger can learn from the seller page? Pick all that apply.","opts":["Show a business phone number instead of a home number","Hide the exact street address until a buyer is verified","Add the names of the seller's children","Show the seller's daily schedule","Strip hidden photo location data before upload"]}]},{"h":"Write the recommendation","read":"<p>Write a short note back to Dana (three or four sentences).</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q7","type":"write","q":"Using Riley as the example, explain what OSINT is and give two specific changes Riley (or the seller page) should make before launch.","min":40}]}]}},"1.3":{"id":"1.3","title":"Police and the 4th Amendment","back":"../unit-1/index.html","w":{"pts":50,"intro":"You will go through the 1.3 notes one part at a time. Each step names the part to open, then asks questions that only someone who read it can answer. You need 100% to open the assignment, and you can check as many times as you like.","steps":[{"h":"Part 00: Deception is legal","read":"<p>Read the top of the page and <b>00: Core Reality: Deception Is Legal</b>.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the 1.3 notes","href":"../unit-1/1-3-notes.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q1","type":"mc","q":"What does the notes say the 4th Amendment protects you from?","opts":["Being recorded by the police at all","Every kind of arrest by an officer","Unreasonable searches and seizures","Any question a police officer asks"]},{"id":"q2","type":"mc","q":"In Georgia and most of the U.S., what can police legally do during questioning?","opts":["Only question you when a lawyer is in the room","Only talk to you for ten minutes at a time at most","Only ask questions that are completely and fully true","Use trickery, bluffing and even lies to get a confession"]},{"id":"q3","type":"mc","q":"What are interrogations designed to do, according to the notes?","opts":["Secure a confession, not hold a casual, honest chat","Help you decide whether you need an attorney","Prove to the officer that you are innocent","Explain your rights to you step by step"]}]},{"h":"Part 01: The \"voluntary\" standard","read":"<p>Read <b>01: The \"Voluntary\" Standard</b>, both lists and \"The pattern\" box.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the 1.3 notes","href":"../unit-1/1-3-notes.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q4","type":"text","q":"Which Georgia law draws the line between allowed tactics and an involuntary confession? Type the section number, like 12-3-456.","ph":"Section number"},{"id":"q5","type":"multi","q":"Which of these tactics can police legally use, according to the notes?","opts":["Posing as a peer, buyer or seller without saying they are police","Claiming your DNA or fingerprints were found at the scene when they were not","Offering a lighter sentence if you talk","Threatening physical harm to get an answer","Claiming a friend already blamed you when nobody said anything"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mc","q":"What is an illegal \"hope of benefit\"?","opts":["Letting the suspect call a parent to ask for help","Offering a lighter sentence in exchange for talking","Telling the suspect the evidence is very strong now","Hoping that the suspect will tell the whole truth"]},{"id":"q7","type":"mc","q":"Which sentence matches \"The pattern\" in the notes?","opts":["Police may lie about anything in the room except evidence","Lying about consequences is allowed if the lie is brief","Lying about facts is allowed; lying about consequences is not","Lying about facts is never allowed in any interview room"]},{"id":"q8","type":"mc","q":"Which action ends the line for police even though you have not confessed?","opts":["Telling you that the case against you is quite strong","Showing you a photograph of a person who is a suspect","Asking you where you were on the night of last Friday","Questioning you after you clearly invoke your rights"]}]},{"h":"Part 02: Five common arenas","read":"<p>Read <b>02: Five Common Interrogation Arenas</b>. Each box has its own rule.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the 1.3 notes","href":"../unit-1/1-3-notes.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q9","type":"match","q":"Match each arena to the rule the notes gives for it.","left":["On school grounds","Traffic stop","At your door (knock and talk)","Undercover or online","Station interrogation room"],"right":["The highest-stakes arena, with the full use of pressure tactics","You never have to let them in without a warrant","Administrators have lower search rules than police","Police do not have to say they are police, even if asked","You owe only ID, registration and insurance"]},{"id":"q10","type":"mc","q":"At a knock and talk, what does the notes say about things officers see once they are inside?","opts":["What they see in plain view can be used","They must close their eyes until a warrant arrives","It can only be used after you say yes","Nothing they see can ever be used"]}]},{"h":"Part 03: How to invoke your rights","read":"<p>Read <b>03: How to Invoke Your Rights</b>, the four steps and the paragraph under them.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the 1.3 notes","href":"../unit-1/1-3-notes.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q11","type":"order","q":"Put the four steps in the order the notes shows them.","items":["Say clearly that you invoke your right to remain silent and want a lawyer","Stop talking: do not explain, guess or make small talk","All legal questioning must stop","Police questioning begins"]},{"id":"q12","type":"text","q":"Type the sentence the notes tells you to say. You can leave out punctuation.","ph":"The exact sentence"},{"id":"q13","type":"mc","q":"What is \"the part almost everyone gets wrong\"?","opts":["You must say it in a loud voice to count","Talking again can un-invoke your rights","You must wait until the officer finishes","You can only invoke your rights once in your life"]},{"id":"q14","type":"write","q":"In your own words, explain why the notes says the safest thing to say in an interrogation is also the shortest. (2 to 3 sentences)","min":25}]}]},"a":{"pts":100,"company":"Discord","color":"#5865F2","tagline":"You are a safety-education intern. Your team is writing a short \"know your rights\" guide for teen users about police and online chats.","from":"Sam Reyes, Safety Education Lead","subject":"Draft the teen \"know your rights\" card (due Friday)","email":"<p>Hi, we are building a help-center card that explains what police can and cannot do when they talk to a teenager, online or in person. It is for Georgia, so use the Georgia rules from class.</p><p>First I need you to check our draft facts, then write the part teens will actually remember. This is a classroom simulation, so the details are made up.</p>","steps":[{"h":"Check the draft facts","read":"<p>The draft card lists six police tactics. Decide which are legal.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q1","type":"multi","q":"Which of these tactics may police legally use in Georgia? Pick all that apply.","opts":["Promising a lighter sentence if the teen confesses","Continuing to question after the teen asks for a lawyer","Telling a teen that a friend already named them","Threatening to hurt the teen unless they answer","Joining a chat as a \"new friend\" without saying they are police","Telling a teen their fingerprints were found at the scene when they were not"]},{"id":"q2","type":"mc","q":"A teen reads: \"If you talk, the officer can promise you will go home today.\" What is wrong with this line?","opts":["It is true only at traffic stops","Officers may only promise freedom in writing","Promising a benefit is an illegal \"hope of benefit\"","Nothing; officers may promise anything"]}]},{"h":"Name the arena","read":"<p>The card has a short section for each place a teen might be questioned.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q3","type":"match","q":"Match each situation to the arena it belongs to.","left":["An officer knocks on the front door and asks to come in","A driver is pulled over for a broken tail light","A \"fellow gamer\" in a chat turns out to be an officer","An assistant principal looks through a backpack"],"right":["Undercover or online","School grounds","Traffic stop","Knock and talk"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mc","q":"In the driver's case, which items does the driver have to provide?","opts":["Permission to search the trunk","A full written statement","Only the car keys","ID, registration and insurance"]}]},{"h":"The script","read":"<p>The card's most important box is the script a teen should say. Sam wants the steps in order and the exact words.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q5","type":"order","q":"Put the four steps of the card in the right order.","items":["Say clearly: I am invoking my right to remain silent, and I want a lawyer","Legal questioning must stop","Stop talking and do not explain","Questioning begins"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mc","q":"A teen says the script, then starts chatting with the officer to \"clear things up.\" What happens to the protection?","opts":["The teen un-invoked their rights by talking again","A lawyer arrives automatically and takes over the talk","The officer must stop asking questions forever anyway","Nothing at all, since the rights last for life once said"]}]},{"h":"Write the card","read":"<p>Write the short text for the card (three or four sentences).</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q7","type":"write","q":"Write the text of the card for teens. Say what lying is allowed, what is not, and give the exact sentence to say.","min":40}]}]}},"1.4":{"id":"1.4","title":"Your Phone's Invisible Footprint","back":"../unit-1/index.html","w":{"pts":50,"intro":"You will go through the 1.4 lesson page and the interactive case files. In the case files you open each case, step through its evidence, and play the match game. You need 100% to open the assignment, and you can check as many times as you like.","steps":[{"h":"The lesson page","read":"<p>Open the 1.4 lesson page. Read the bell ringer and the \"Notes and Examples\" box under it.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the 1.4 lesson page","href":"../unit-1/1-4-your-phones-invisible-footprint.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q1","type":"multi","q":"Which three categories does the 1.4 page use to organize what your phone gives away?","opts":["Location","Battery life","Hidden data","Habits","Screen size"]},{"id":"q2","type":"mc","q":"How many people did BTK kill between 1974 and 1991, according to the bell ringer?","opts":["Seven","Ten","Four","Fourteen"]},{"id":"q3","type":"mc","q":"What mistake does the bell ringer say led to BTK's arrest in 2005?","opts":["A confession that he made to a friend by phone","A fingerprint left on a stolen car at a scene","A floppy disk whose hidden file data pointed to him","A photo of him that ran in a local newspaper"]}]},{"h":"Case 1: BTK (Dennis Rader)","read":"<p>Open the case files and choose <b>Dennis Rader (BTK)</b>. Step through all six steps on the <b>The evidence</b> tab and read the \"What he did not think about\" tab.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the case files","href":"../unit-1/caught-by-their-data.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q4","type":"mc","q":"How did police answer BTK's question about whether a floppy disk could be traced?","opts":["With a phone call to the TV station","With a letter from a lawyer to his home","By refusing to answer at all","With a newspaper ad saying it would be OK"]},{"id":"q5","type":"text","q":"The file's hidden properties named a church. Type the name of the church.","ph":"Name of the church"},{"id":"q6","type":"text","q":"What word do we use for hidden data about a file, such as who last saved it?","ph":"One word"},{"id":"q7","type":"order","q":"Put the BTK steps in order.","items":["Investigators read the file's hidden properties","The purple floppy disk arrives","A church website shows the council president is Dennis Rader","He asks whether a floppy disk can be traced","DNA confirms the match","He is arrested"]},{"id":"q8","type":"mc","q":"Why was it legal for police to tell him \"it will be OK\" when it was not true?","opts":["Police may lie to suspects, as lesson 1.3 showed","The ad was a private message, not a statement","He had already confessed on the phone","The law only covers lies told in a courtroom"]}]},{"h":"Case 2: Bryan Kohberger","read":"<p>Choose <b>Bryan Kohberger</b>. Step through the evidence and read the \"What he did not think about\" tab.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the case files","href":"../unit-1/caught-by-their-data.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q9","type":"mc","q":"In which window of time did his phone not connect to cell towers near the home?","opts":["About noon to 2 p.m.","About 3 a.m. to 5 a.m.","About 9 a.m. to 11 a.m.","About 7 p.m. to 9 p.m."]},{"id":"q10","type":"mc","q":"What does the case file mean by \"an absence is also data\"?","opts":["A deleted app erases every one of its records for good","A missing text message can never be recovered by anyone","A phone that stops connecting leaves a gap in the log","A phone that is turned off cannot be traced by any means"]},{"id":"q11","type":"text","q":"In which month and year did the four University of Idaho students die? Type it as month and year.","ph":"Month and year"},{"id":"q12","type":"mc","q":"What did the phone data do on its own in the case?","opts":["It was ignored by investigators because it was a gap","It replaced the need for a warrant in every county","It proved the whole case against him all by itself","It fit together with the car, the DNA and earlier visits"]}]},{"h":"Match the evidence","read":"<p>Open the other six case files and read each one. Then open the <b>Match the evidence</b> tab and play the game.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the case files","href":"../unit-1/caught-by-their-data.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q13","type":"match","q":"Match each case to the data that caught or cleared the person.","left":["BTK (Rader)","Kohberger","Golden State Killer","Ross Ulbricht","John McAfee","Reality Winner","Richard Dabate","Rodney Bradford"],"right":["GPS coordinates inside a photo","Tracking dots printed on a page","A cousin's DNA on a genealogy site","A Facebook server record","An old forum post with an email address","A step counter on a wrist","Cell-tower records: a gap and a pattern","Hidden file properties on a floppy disk"]},{"id":"q14","type":"mc","q":"What two things do the printer tracking dots in the Reality Winner case encode?","opts":["The printer's serial number and the date and time","The text of the page and the name of the person who read it","The paper brand and the price of the ink that was used","The name of the user who printed it and her home address"]},{"id":"q15","type":"mc","q":"What made the Rodney Bradford case different from the others?","opts":["Nobody was ever arrested","The data cleared an innocent person","It involved a printer and a laser","The data was wrong and was thrown out"]}]},{"h":"The fair warning","read":"<p>Read the last section, \"The fair-warning part: this is about you too,\" and the five audit questions.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the case files","href":"../unit-1/caught-by-their-data.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q16","type":"write","q":"Pick one of the three categories (Location, Habits, Hidden data) and explain, in your own words, how it could be used against an ordinary person. Give one example from the case files. (2 to 3 sentences)","min":25}]}]},"a":{"pts":100,"company":"Apple","color":"#1d1d1f","tagline":"You are a privacy-settings intern. The team is writing a short guide to help teens see what their iPhone quietly records.","from":"Jordan Lee, Privacy Education Team","subject":"Audit the three kinds of phone data for the teen guide","email":"<p>Hi, we need a clear, honest guide for teens about the information a phone keeps without being asked. We use three categories: <b>Location</b>, <b>Habits</b> and <b>Hidden data</b>.</p><p>First sort a list, then connect each category to a real case from class, then give advice. This is a classroom simulation, so the details are made up.</p>","steps":[{"h":"Sort the data","read":"<p>Decide which of the three categories each item belongs to.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q1","type":"match","q":"Match each item to its category.","left":["Cell-tower connections near a home","The apps you open every night at 11 p.m.","GPS coordinates saved inside a photo file"],"right":["Location","Hidden data","Habits"]},{"id":"q2","type":"mc","q":"A teen turns off location tagging for the camera app. Which category does this shrink?","opts":["Only the habits category and never anything about photos","Only the cell-tower records that the carrier keeps","Hidden data in the photos, and the location in them","Nothing at all, since photos never hold any locations"]}]},{"h":"Connect the cases","read":"<p>The guide uses real cases as warnings. Match each case to the lesson it teaches.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q3","type":"match","q":"Match each case to the lesson it teaches teens.","left":["BTK","Kohberger","Golden State Killer","Rodney Bradford"],"right":["A missing signal is also evidence","Files carry hidden metadata you never type","A trail can protect the innocent","Relatives' choices can reveal your DNA"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mc","q":"A teen says: \"If I turn my phone off, I disappear.\" Which case shows the problem with that idea?","opts":["BTK: a floppy disk with a hidden file was mailed in","Bradford: a post on a website proved he was at home","Winner: a color printer added tiny dots to the page","Kohberger: the carrier logged the connections around it"]}]},{"h":"Advice to teens","read":"<p>Jordan wants two concrete tips and one honest warning.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q5","type":"multi","q":"Which tips belong in the guide? Pick all that fit what you learned.","opts":["Search yourself the way an investigator would","Turn off location tagging for the camera","Check what an app strips from photos before posting an original","Post the original file so nothing gets lost","Delete the cell-phone plan to avoid logs"]},{"id":"q6","type":"write","q":"Write the guide's short warning (three or four sentences). Name the three categories, give one real case, and say why this matters even for people who did nothing wrong.","min":45}]}]}},"1.5":{"id":"1.5","title":"How Strong Is Your Password?","back":"../unit-1/index.html","w":{"pts":50,"intro":"You will go through the 1.5 password notes: seven attack terms, three defenses and the password lab at the bottom of the page. The lab questions need you to actually type into it. You need 100% to open the assignment, and you can check as many times as you like.","steps":[{"h":"The attacks","read":"<p>Open the notes and read the left side, <b>How Attackers Crack Passwords</b>. Read every card.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the 1.5 notes","href":"../unit-1/1-5-notes.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q1","type":"match","q":"Match each attack term to what the notes says it is.","left":["Brute-force attack","Dictionary attack","Credential stuffing","Keylogger","Hash and rainbow table"],"right":["Trying one site's leaked username and password on many other sites","Software that secretly records every key you press","Trying every possible combination, one by one, until one works","A lookup chart of pre-computed scrambled versions of common passwords","Starting from real words and leaked lists, then adding predictable tweaks"]},{"id":"q2","type":"mc","q":"According to the notes, what actually decides how many guesses an attacker needs?","opts":["Entropy: how unpredictable the password is","How complicated the password looks to a person","How long ago the password was last changed","How many symbols the password happens to have"]},{"id":"q3","type":"mc","q":"Why does leetspeak (P@ssw0rd!) barely slow an attacker down?","opts":["Attackers cannot type symbols into login pages","Cracking tools check those swaps automatically","Hashes ignore any characters that are not letters","Sites reject passwords that contain an at-sign"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mc","q":"Why is no password \"strong enough\" to beat a keylogger?","opts":["Keyloggers can only be used on short passwords","A strong password is blocked by the keylogger","The attacker never has to guess; they read what you typed","Keyloggers only work when a site has no hashing"]},{"id":"q5","type":"text","q":"Sites do not store your actual password. They store a scrambled version called a what?","ph":"One word"}]},{"h":"The defenses","read":"<p>Read the right side, <b>What Actually Defends You</b>, and the closing line about \"7 ways in, 3 ways to actually stop them.\"</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the 1.5 notes","href":"../unit-1/1-5-notes.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q6","type":"match","q":"Match each defense to what the notes says it does.","left":["MFA","Passphrase","Password manager"],"right":["A second proof of identity beyond the password, such as a code on your phone","Makes and remembers a unique random password for every site","A string of random, unrelated words instead of one word with substitutions"]},{"id":"q7","type":"mc","q":"Which attack does a password manager solve, according to the notes?","opts":["The problem of keyloggers that record every key you press","The problem of sites that keep no passwords on file at all","The problem of rainbow tables being published on the internet","The reuse problem that makes credential stuffing possible"]},{"id":"q8","type":"mc","q":"The notes count how many ways in and how many ways to stop them. What are the counts?","opts":["7 ways in and 3 ways to stop them","10 ways in and 1 way to stop them","3 ways in and 7 ways to stop them","5 ways in and 5 ways to stop them"]},{"id":"q9","type":"mc","q":"What does the notes say beats trying to out-clever any single attack?","opts":["Hiding your username on every single site that you use","Layering a passphrase, a password manager and MFA together","Picking one very long and complex password for every site","Changing your one password every single week of the year"]}]},{"h":"The password lab","read":"<p>Scroll to <b>Try it: crack your own made-up password</b>. Use made-up passwords only, never a real one. Type each test password into the lab and read what it says.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the 1.5 notes","href":"../unit-1/1-5-notes.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q10","type":"mc","q":"Type <code>P@ssw0rd!</code> into the lab. What does it say?","opts":["It needs about 4 days of nonstop guessing to crack","It needs more than a billion years of guessing to crack","Cracked instantly: a common word with simple tricks","It cannot be tested at all because it contains a symbol"]},{"id":"q11","type":"num","q":"Type <code>Qx7#vLm2</code> into the lab. About how many bits does it report?","ph":"A whole number"},{"id":"q12","type":"num","q":"Type <code>river-comet-anchor-maple</code> (four random words) into the lab. About how many bits does it report?","ph":"A whole number"},{"id":"q13","type":"mc","q":"What does comparing those two results show?","opts":["Short passwords are always better than any long passwords","Symbols are always better than a string of random words","Words can never be as strong as a group of symbols","Four random words are about as strong as 8 random characters"]},{"id":"q14","type":"write","q":"In your own words, explain why layering a passphrase, a password manager and MFA beats trying to make one clever password. (2 to 3 sentences)","min":25}]}]},"a":{"pts":100,"company":"Netflix","color":"#E50914","tagline":"You are a security intern on the accounts team. Your lead wants a plan to stop stolen passwords from turning into stolen accounts.","from":"Priya Natarajan, Accounts Security Lead","subject":"Why are accounts getting taken over? Review three incident reports","email":"<p>Hi, we are seeing viewers lose access to their accounts even though our own servers were never hacked. I need you to label what is happening and recommend defenses.</p><p>This is a classroom simulation, so the details are made up. Work in order.</p>","steps":[{"h":"Name the attack","read":"<p>For each report, choose the attack that fits best.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q1","type":"match","q":"Match each report to the attack.","left":["A viewer reused the same password on a gaming site that was breached, and criminals logged in to the streaming account with it.","A program tries thousands of real words, each with a capital letter and the number 123 added.","Hidden software on a family computer sends everything the viewer types to a stranger.","A program tries every possible combination of four characters."],"right":["Dictionary attack","Brute-force attack","Keylogger","Credential stuffing"]}]},{"h":"Choose the best defense","read":"<p>Priya wants the single best fix for each problem.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q2","type":"mc","q":"Viewers reuse one password on many sites. Which tool fixes that most directly?","opts":["A password manager that makes a unique password per site","Changing the same password once every single year","A password with an at-sign swapped in for the letter a","A longer password that is reused on every site"]},{"id":"q3","type":"mc","q":"An attacker already has a viewer's exact password. Which defense can still stop the login?","opts":["Making the username much longer than before","MFA, a second proof such as a code on the phone","Replacing every letter o in the password with a zero","Adding a capital letter to the end of the password"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mc","q":"A viewer thinks <code>P@ssw0rd!</code> is clever. What is the best response?","opts":["It is strong, because it has a capital letter","It is weak only if the viewer writes it down","Cracking tools check those swaps automatically, so it is weak","It is strong, because it has four kinds of characters"]}]},{"h":"Use the lab","read":"<p>Open the <a href=\"../unit-1/1-5-notes.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">password lab in the 1.5 notes</a> and test the four made-up passwords below. Use only these made-up passwords.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q5","type":"mc","q":"Test <code>P@ssw0rd!</code>, <code>kittycat</code>, <code>Qx7#vLm2</code> and <code>river-comet-anchor-maple-tiger</code>. Which one does the lab say takes the longest to crack?","opts":["kittycat","Qx7#vLm2","P@ssw0rd!","river-comet-anchor-maple-tiger"]},{"id":"q6","type":"num","q":"How many bits does the lab report for <code>river-comet-anchor-maple-tiger</code>?","ph":"A whole number"}]},{"h":"Write the recommendation","read":"<p>Write the plan Priya will send to viewers (three or four sentences).</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q7","type":"write","q":"Write the advice for viewers. Name at least two defenses, say which attack each one stops, and say why a password with symbol swaps is not enough.","min":45}]}]}},"1.6":{"id":"1.6","title":"How Hackers Track You Online","back":"../unit-1/index.html","w":{"pts":50,"intro":"You will go through the 1.6 notes, \"Who's Watching You,\" one section at a time. One step needs you to run the ad auction demo on the page. You need 100% to open the assignment, and you can check as many times as you like.","steps":[{"h":"The three layers","read":"<p>Open the notes and read the top of the page down to <b>1: The hardware</b>. The page splits tracking into three layers.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the 1.6 notes","href":"../unit-1/whos-watching-you.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q1","type":"match","q":"Match each layer to what the notes says it is.","left":["Layer 1: Hardware","Layer 2: Software","Layer 3: The market"],"right":["The apps you install and the permissions you give them","Brokers who buy and resell data about you","Built-in sensors that are always on"]},{"id":"q2","type":"mc","q":"Which layer does the page say most people only think about?","opts":["The middle one, the apps and their permissions","The first one, the phone's sensors","The last one, the data market","None of them, since people never think about it"]}]},{"h":"1: The hardware","read":"<p>Read section <b>1: The hardware</b>. Every sensor card has \"Why it's there\" and \"What it can do to you.\"</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the 1.6 notes","href":"../unit-1/whos-watching-you.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q3","type":"match","q":"Match each sensor to what the page says it can do to you.","left":["LiDAR scanner","Barometer","TrueDepth camera","Ambient light sensor","Accelerometer"],"right":["Reads micro-expressions to see whether an ad made you happy","Scans the 3D layout and furniture of your bedroom","Estimates your family's income from the floor you live on","Tells an app whether you are in bed or outside","Identifies you by the rhythm of your walk"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mc","q":"What reason does the page give for the barometer being in the phone?","opts":["It measures how fast your phone is moving through the air","It tells 911 what floor you are on inside a tall building","It checks that your charger is a genuine one","It helps your phone find free Wi-Fi inside a building"]},{"id":"q5","type":"mc","q":"According to the notes, what does the accelerometer data let a researcher do?","opts":["Read the private messages that the person sends each day","Tell exactly which apps the person has installed on it","Identify a specific person from their walking pattern","Find the password that the person types in at login"]}]},{"h":"2: The apps","read":"<p>Read section <b>2: The apps</b>. Notice what the page says the policy says, and what it admits the policy does not say.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the 1.6 notes","href":"../unit-1/whos-watching-you.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q6","type":"mc","q":"What does the notes say TikTok's U.S. privacy policy says it may collect from the videos you make?","opts":["Your exact password for every one of your other apps","Your bank account number and your card number","Your contacts' private text messages and photos","Biometric identifiers such as faceprints and voiceprints"]},{"id":"q7","type":"mc","q":"What does the page tell you to do before you hand an app your face and voice?","opts":["Read the app's privacy policy","Turn on airplane mode first","Delete your account first","Ask a friend to try it first"]},{"id":"q8","type":"text","q":"What does the page say you should check on Instagram's map feature, because it is easy to leave on? Type one word.","ph":"One word"}]},{"h":"3: The market","read":"<p>Read section <b>3: The market</b>. Then scroll down to <b>Try it: watch an ad auction</b>, press the button and read the result.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the 1.6 notes","href":"../unit-1/whos-watching-you.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q9","type":"num","q":"The page says a 2022 study estimated a typical American's data is exposed in ad auctions about how many times a day?"},{"id":"q10","type":"num","q":"In the ad auction demo, how many companies receive your location and ad ID?"},{"id":"q11","type":"text","q":"In the demo, which company wins the ad slot?","ph":"Company name"},{"id":"q12","type":"mc","q":"In the pharmacy example, what happens to the bidders who lose the auction?","opts":["They have to delete the data at once","They may keep the data they received","They are fined by the government","They are told whether they lost"]},{"id":"q13","type":"order","q":"Put the warrant loophole in order.","items":["The government buys the data from the broker","No warrant was ever required","Your app sells your location data to a broker"]}]},{"h":"4 and 5: Lock it down and see it yourself","read":"<p>Read section <b>4: Lock it down</b>, then <b>5: See it yourself</b>, including the Incognito myth.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the 1.6 notes","href":"../unit-1/whos-watching-you.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q14","type":"match","q":"Match each setting to what it switches off or limits.","left":["Precise Location off for social apps","Motion and Fitness off for social apps","Allow Apps to Request to Track off","Audit every permission"],"right":["Kills the advertising ID that feeds the auction","Shuts down identifying you by your walk","Keeps apps from learning your exact room","Removes access an app does not need to work"]},{"id":"q15","type":"mc","q":"According to the notes, what was Incognito mode built to hide?","opts":["Your identity from every one of the ad networks","Your exact location from every website you visit","Your history from other people using your computer","Your browser fingerprint from the trackers on a site"]},{"id":"q16","type":"mc","q":"Why does the page warn you to use the exact CreepJS address and no other?","opts":["Other addresses are blocked by every school","The real address is the only one that loads fast","The developer charges a fee at other addresses","Copycat sites run modified code to capture your fingerprint"]},{"id":"q17","type":"write","q":"In your own words, explain the idea behind \"If the app is free, the price is the map of your life.\" Use one example from the page. (2 to 3 sentences)","min":25}]}]},"a":{"pts":100,"company":"Instagram","color":"#C13584","tagline":"You are a product-safety intern. The team is reviewing a map feature that shows teens' friends where they are.","from":"Alex Rivera, Product Safety Manager","subject":"Review the new location sharing screen before it ships","email":"<p>Hi, our new map feature lets a teen share where they are with friends. Before launch I want you to look at the data it touches and how the settings should work.</p><p>Use what you learned about the three layers of tracking. This is a classroom simulation, so the details are made up.</p>","steps":[{"h":"Which layer is it?","read":"<p>Sort each piece of the feature into the layer it belongs to.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q1","type":"match","q":"Match each part of the feature to its layer.","left":["The phone's built-in sensors that detect where you are","The permission screen that asks to turn on location","A company that buys location data and resells it"],"right":["The market","Software","Hardware"]},{"id":"q2","type":"mc","q":"A teen's phone says \"Precise Location: On\" for the map. What does precise location give the app?","opts":["Your exact spot, not just your general area","Only the name of your cell carrier","Only the country that you live in","Nothing, because maps do not use location"]}]},{"h":"What can go wrong?","read":"<p>Alex wants three risks explained in plain words.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q3","type":"mc","q":"A teen opens the app in a doctor's waiting room, and the location goes into an ad auction. What may happen to the bidders who lose?","opts":["Only the winner of the auction can ever see the data","They may keep the data, so more companies know where they were","The data is deleted the moment the auction ends","Nobody sees the data until a court orders that it be shared"]},{"id":"q4","type":"order","q":"A government agency wants a teen's location history. Put the steps of the warrant loophole in order.","items":["The agency buys the data from the broker","An app sells location data to a broker","No warrant is ever required"]},{"id":"q5","type":"mc","q":"Why is a phone's walking rhythm a privacy risk?","opts":["It reveals the passwords that you use for your accounts","It shows the exact words that you speak out loud","It can identify a specific person, like a fingerprint","It always matches every person in exactly the same way"]}]},{"h":"Set the defaults","read":"<p>Pick the safe default settings for the launch.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q6","type":"multi","q":"Which settings should be off by default for teens? Pick all that apply.","opts":["Precise location sharing with friends","Tracking across other apps","The ability to report a problem","Motion and Fitness access","The account password"]},{"id":"q7","type":"write","q":"Write the launch recommendation (three or four sentences). Name two risks from the tracking layers, give one safe default setting, and say why \"the app is free\" matters.","min":45}]}]}},"1.7":{"id":"1.7","title":"Copyright and Ethics","back":"../unit-1/index.html","w":{"pts":50,"intro":"You will go through the Copyright Case File one case at a time. Every step names the case to open, then asks questions that only someone who read it can answer. You need 100% to open the assignment, and you can check as many times as you like.","steps":[{"h":"Case 01: What copyright covers","read":"<p>Open the case file and read the top of the page and <b>Case 01: What copyright actually covers</b>.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the 1.7 case file","href":"../unit-1/copyright-case-file.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q1","type":"mc","q":"When does your work become protected by copyright?","opts":["After you register it with a government office","After one year of public use of the work","After you put the copyright symbol on it","The moment you make it, with no paperwork or fee"]},{"id":"q2","type":"multi","q":"Which of these does the notes say are NOT protected by copyright?","opts":["An idea alone, like a story idea about a superhero","Code you wrote for a game","Facts such as the sky is blue","A photo you took","Titles, names or short phrases"]},{"id":"q3","type":"mc","q":"What exactly does copyright protect?","opts":["The exact words, notes or pixels, not the idea behind them","The idea behind your work, in any form that it takes","Only work that has already been sold to the public","Any other work that is about the same general subject"]},{"id":"q4","type":"match","q":"Match each exclusive right to what it means.","left":["Copy it","Share it","Display it","Perform it","Remix it"],"right":["Change it into something new (a derivative work)","Play or perform it out loud","Distribute or post it","Make more copies","Show it publicly"]}]},{"h":"Case 02: The fair use test","read":"<p>Read <b>Case 02: The fair use test</b>, the four factors and the table of real posts.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the 1.7 case file","href":"../unit-1/copyright-case-file.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q5","type":"match","q":"Match each fair use factor to the question it asks.","left":["Factor 1","Factor 2","Factor 3","Factor 4"],"right":["Does your use hurt sales of the original?","How much of the work did you use?","Why are you using it? Is it transformative?","What kind of work is it, factual or creative?"]},{"id":"q6","type":"mc","q":"Which factor does the notes say courts and platforms weigh the heaviest?","opts":["Factor 3: how much you used","Factor 4: does it hurt sales","Factor 1: why you are using it","Factor 2: what kind of work it is"]},{"id":"q7","type":"mc","q":"How does the notes rate a movie reaction video that plays almost an entire 20-minute video?","opts":["Public domain","Usually fair use","Usually not fair use","Always fair use"]},{"id":"q8","type":"mc","q":"Which is usually fair use, according to the table?","opts":["Reposting an artist's picture online without giving any credit","A full song used as background music, completely unedited","Posting someone else's whole video and calling it your own","A review with a few short clips and your own analysis"]}]},{"h":"Case 03: Public domain and Creative Commons","read":"<p>Read <b>Case 03: Public domain and Creative Commons</b>, including the four license cards.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the 1.7 case file","href":"../unit-1/copyright-case-file.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q9","type":"mc","q":"Why can you use any NASA image for free?","opts":["U.S. federal government work is public domain","NASA images all use the CC BY-NC license","NASA owns every image but promises it never sues anyone","All images taken in outer space are public domain"]},{"id":"q10","type":"match","q":"Match each Creative Commons license to what it requires.","left":["CC BY","CC BY-SA","CC BY-NC","CC0"],"right":["Credit the creator by name","Credit the creator and do not use it to make money","Credit the creator and share your remix under the same license","The creator gives up every right; use it however you want"]},{"id":"q11","type":"mc","q":"What does \"royalty-free\" usually mean, according to the notes?","opts":["The work is free to use for anything","You pay once instead of paying every time","The work is in the public domain","The creator cannot ever be paid"]},{"id":"q12","type":"mc","q":"Which of these is a legit free-to-use source named in the notes?","opts":["Google Images","A random fan page","Wikimedia Commons","A friend's camera roll"]}]},{"h":"Cases 04 to 06: Mistakes, plagiarism and consequences","read":"<p>Read <b>Case 04: Where teens actually break the rules</b>, <b>Case 05: Plagiarism vs. copyright</b> and <b>Case 06: What happens if you get it wrong</b>.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the 1.7 case file","href":"../unit-1/copyright-case-file.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q13","type":"mc","q":"A teen posts a video with a trending song from TikTok. They then download the video and repost it elsewhere. Why is the music no longer covered?","opts":["Music licenses on every app expire after exactly one day","Songs are in the public domain on all other sites","The teen must pay the platform a fee to reuse the song","The platform's license does not travel with the video"]},{"id":"q14","type":"mc","q":"How does the notes rate fan art and fan fiction?","opts":["Technically infringement, but usually tolerated until it is sold","Always fully legal, because it is covered by fair use","Never tolerated by any creator or any studio at all","Public domain automatically after exactly one full year"]},{"id":"q15","type":"mc","q":"What does the U.S. Copyright Office say about a fully AI-generated image?","opts":["It belongs to the person who typed the prompt","It cannot be copyrighted by anyone","It belongs to the company that made the tool","It is protected for 95 years"]},{"id":"q16","type":"match","q":"Match each problem to what solves it.","left":["Plagiarism","Copyright infringement","Takedown on a platform"],"right":["Permission, a license, or a fair use argument","Citing your source","The copyright holder files a DMCA claim"]},{"id":"q17","type":"write","q":"In your own words, explain why citing a source stops plagiarism but does not stop copyright infringement. (2 to 3 sentences)","min":25}]}]},"a":{"pts":100,"company":"YouTube","color":"#FF0000","tagline":"You are a creator-support intern. Your team helps young creators avoid takedowns, mutes and strikes.","from":"Marcus Bell, Creator Support Lead","subject":"Four creator questions for this week's help article","email":"<p>Hi, I am writing a short help article for teen creators. Each question below came from a real kind of post. I need you to give the right answer so the article is accurate.</p><p>This is a classroom simulation, so the posts are made up.</p>","steps":[{"h":"Fair use check","read":"<p>Use the four factors: why you used it, what kind of work it is, how much you used, and whether it hurts sales.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q1","type":"match","q":"Match each post to the likely verdict from the notes.","left":["A short parody over a few clips with your own jokes","Almost an entire 20-minute video with a few comments","A cropped movie screenshot with a joke caption"],"right":["Usually not fair use","A grey area, usually tolerated","Usually fair use"]},{"id":"q2","type":"mc","q":"A creator uses a full song, unedited, as background music in a public video. Which factor hurts them most?","opts":["Factor 1, because music is never transformative","None, because the song is popular","Factor 3, because they used the whole thing","Factor 2, because songs are always facts"]}]},{"h":"Which license?","read":"<p>Marcus has four photos that creators want to reuse.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q3","type":"match","q":"Match each situation to the right license or status.","left":["A photo you may use if you credit the photographer, even for a paid ad","A photo free for a school video but not for a paid ad","A photo made by a NASA employee as part of their job","A photo the creator waived every right on, on purpose"],"right":["Public domain (U.S. government work)","CC0","CC BY","CC BY-NC"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mc","q":"A stock site says \"royalty-free.\" What should a creator do?","opts":["Assume that it is in the public domain already","Credit the site and then ignore the license terms","Use it anywhere at all with no limits on it","Read the actual license, because royalty-free is not free"]}]},{"h":"Plagiarism or copyright?","read":"<p>One help-article section explains the difference. Marcus wants the cases sorted.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q5","type":"match","q":"Match each case to the problem it creates.","left":["A student copies three paragraphs of a Wikipedia article into an essay without a citation","A creator reposts another artist's work with a credit but no permission","A creator reposts another artist's work and says it is theirs"],"right":["Copyright infringement","Both problems at once","Plagiarism at school"]},{"id":"q6","type":"multi","q":"Which are real consequences a platform can apply to a creator who gets copyright wrong?","opts":["A DMCA takedown of the post","The platform deletes the creator's family photos","The platform sends a bill to the creator's school","Account strikes and a possible ban","Demonetization or a muted audio track"]}]},{"h":"Write the answer","read":"<p>Marcus needs a short answer to publish (three or four sentences).</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q7","type":"write","q":"A teen asks: \"Can I use a clip of my favorite show in my video?\" Answer using at least two fair use factors, and mention one safer option.","min":45}]}]}},"1.8":{"id":"1.8","title":"Hacking Ethics","back":"../unit-1/index.html","w":{"pts":50,"intro":"You will go through the Rules of Engagement briefing one part at a time. One step needs you to play the color game on the page. You need 100% to open the assignment, and you can check as many times as you like.","steps":[{"h":"The big idea","read":"<p>Open the briefing and read the top of the page, down to \"Every Crime Has a Category.\"</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the 1.8 briefing","href":"../unit-1/hacking-ethics-briefing.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q1","type":"mc","q":"According to the briefing, what decides whether the same computer skill is a paid job or a federal crime?","opts":["Whether someone with real authority said yes first","Whether anything was actually damaged or taken","Whether the person works for a big company","How advanced the technical skill is"]}]},{"h":"Part 00: Every crime has a category","read":"<p>Read <b>00: Every Crime Has a Category</b>. It uses killing and stealing as examples, then connects them to hacking.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the 1.8 briefing","href":"../unit-1/hacking-ethics-briefing.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q2","type":"match","q":"Match each degree of killing to its description.","left":["First-degree murder","Second-degree murder","Voluntary manslaughter","Involuntary manslaughter"],"right":["Intentional but formed in the moment, not planned","Intentional in the heat of the moment after real provocation","No intent to kill, caused by reckless or careless behavior","Intentional and planned out in advance"]},{"id":"q3","type":"match","q":"Match each type of theft to its description.","left":["Petty theft","Grand theft","Robbery","Burglary"],"right":["Taking property from a person using force or the threat of force","Taking property above that same cutoff","Entering a building with intent to commit a crime, even if nothing is taken","Taking property below a state's dollar cutoff"]},{"id":"q4","type":"match","q":"Match each color to the access it describes.","left":["Green","Amber","Red"],"right":["Grey area: nobody meant harm but nobody officially said yes","Unauthorized: nobody said yes, whatever the reason","Authorized: somebody said yes in writing before anything started"]},{"id":"q5","type":"mc","q":"The briefing says unauthorized computer access works \"exactly the same way\" as what?","opts":["Contracts, where the signature is always optional","Homicide and theft, where category changes the penalty","Copyright, where giving credit changes the law itself","Traffic tickets, where every single case is the same"]}]},{"h":"Part 01: White, grey and black hats","read":"<p>Read <b>01: White hat, grey hat, black hat</b>.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the 1.8 briefing","href":"../unit-1/hacking-ethics-briefing.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q6","type":"match","q":"Match each hat to its description.","left":["White hat","Grey hat","Black hat"],"right":["Breaks in without asking but says they meant well","Breaks in to steal, damage, spy or make illegal money","Hired or invited to find weak spots, and reports them"]},{"id":"q7","type":"mc","q":"What single question does the color of the hat answer?","opts":["Did they get paid?","Did they use a famous tool?","Did they have permission?","Did they succeed?"]},{"id":"q8","type":"mc","q":"Why can a grey hat with good intentions and a black hat with bad ones be charged under the same law?","opts":["Judges treat every hacker in exactly the same way","Grey hats always cause exactly the same damage","Good intentions are only ever a defense in a school","The law cares about permission, not how good your reason was"]},{"id":"q9","type":"mc","q":"In the briefing's example, a grey hat finds a way into a school website and then does what?","opts":["Emails the school to say they found a way in afterward","Asks the school for permission before getting in","Deletes the school's grade records without telling anyone","Sells the way in to a stranger on the internet"]}]},{"h":"Try it: what color is the access?","read":"<p>Scroll to <b>Try it: what color is the access?</b> and classify all eight situations. Then answer based on what you did.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the 1.8 briefing","href":"../unit-1/hacking-ethics-briefing.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q10","type":"num","q":"How many situations are in the color game?"},{"id":"q11","type":"text","q":"In the briefing's example of the Red category, what do you do to a friend's account \"just to look around\"? Type the one-word action.","ph":"One or two words"},{"id":"q12","type":"write","q":"In your own words, explain why a person who \"only looked\" and \"meant well\" can still be breaking the law. (2 to 3 sentences)","min":25}]}]},"a":{"pts":100,"company":"Google","color":"#4285F4","tagline":"You are an intern on a security-research outreach team. Your lead handles messages from people who say they found a problem.","from":"Lena Okafor, Security Outreach Lead","subject":"Sort this week's four security reports by permission","email":"<p>Hi, we got four messages from people who say they found weaknesses in our products. Before anyone replies, I need you to sort each one by permission, because that is what decides how we handle it.</p><p>This is a classroom simulation, so the reports are made up.</p>","steps":[{"h":"Color each report","read":"<p>Use the three colors: green is authorized, amber is a grey area, red is unauthorized.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q1","type":"match","q":"Match each report to its color.","left":["A contractor with a signed agreement tests a new app for two weeks and files a full report","A user stumbles onto a real problem on a website by accident and is not sure whether to mention it","A student guesses a classmate's password \"just to look around\" and changes nothing"],"right":["Amber: a grey area","Green: authorized","Red: unauthorized"]}]},{"h":"Name the hat","read":"<p>Lena also wants the hat for each person.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q2","type":"match","q":"Match each person to the hat.","left":["Paid to try to break into a company's website, then hands over a report","Enters a school site without asking, then emails to say it can be done","Breaks into a store's system to steal credit card numbers"],"right":["Grey hat","White hat","Black hat"]},{"id":"q3","type":"mc","q":"A researcher says: \"I never changed anything, so I did nothing wrong.\" What is the best reply?","opts":["That is a defense only if they emailed the school afterward","The law cares about permission, not how good your reason was","That is fine, because no change at all means no crime","That only matters if the website was a very small one"]}]},{"h":"Same skill, different category","read":"<p>The reply template will use the idea that categories change consequences.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q4","type":"mc","q":"Which pair is the best match for how the law treats categories?","opts":["Petty theft and grand theft are always exactly the same crime","Every killing carries exactly the same prison sentence","Two kinds of killing can carry very different penalties","Burglary only counts when something is actually taken"]},{"id":"q5","type":"write","q":"Write a short reply (three or four sentences) to a person who found a real weakness and tested it without asking. Explain what category they are in, why good intentions do not change that, and what safer step they could take next time.","min":45}]}]}},"1.9":{"id":"1.9","title":"Common Security Problems","back":"../unit-1/index.html","w":{"pts":50,"intro":"You will go through \"The Front Door\" one lock at a time. One step needs you to use the \"change one number\" demo on the page. You need 100% to open the assignment, and you can check as many times as you like.","steps":[{"h":"The four locks","read":"<p>Open the notes and read the top of the page, down to <b>01: HTTPS</b>. Four cards name the four locks.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the 1.9 notes","href":"../unit-1/the-front-door.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q1","type":"match","q":"Match each lock to the building picture the notes uses.","left":["HTTPS","CAPTCHA","2FA","OWASP"],"right":["The building code","The doorman who checks you are a person","The lock on the connection itself","The second deadbolt"]}]},{"h":"01: HTTPS","read":"<p>Read <b>01: HTTPS</b>, including the two address-bar examples and the warning about the padlock.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the 1.9 notes","href":"../unit-1/the-front-door.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q2","type":"mc","q":"According to the notes, what does plain HTTP (no S) send as readable text?","opts":["Only the pictures that appear on the page","Only your username and nothing else at all","Nothing at all, because HTTP is always private","Everything you type, like passwords and messages"]},{"id":"q3","type":"match","q":"Match each thing the padlock checks to what it does.","left":["Encryption","Certificate identity"],"right":["Confirms you are talking to the real website in the address bar","Scrambles data so nobody spying in transit can read it"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mc","q":"What does the notes say a valid padlock does NOT prove?","opts":["That the website itself is honest","That the address is spelled correctly","That your connection is private","That the page loaded at all"]}]},{"h":"02: CAPTCHA","read":"<p>Read <b>02: CAPTCHA</b>, including the five kinds.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the 1.9 notes","href":"../unit-1/the-front-door.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q5","type":"mc","q":"What is a CAPTCHA's entire job, according to the notes?","opts":["To store your login so it is ready for the next visit","To slow down programs pretending to be a real person","To scramble your password while it is in transit","To tell websites exactly who your friends are"]},{"id":"q6","type":"match","q":"Match each kind of CAPTCHA to what it asks.","left":["Distorted text","Image grid","Checkbox","Invisible","Slider or puzzle"],"right":["No test unless your behavior looks suspicious","Click every square with a traffic light or crosswalk","Type the wavy, stretched-out letters and numbers","Drag a piece into the missing spot with a smooth motion","Click I am not a robot while your mouse movement is watched"]},{"id":"q7","type":"multi","q":"Which of these does the notes say bots can do that a human cannot? Pick all that apply.","opts":["Try millions of stolen username and password pairs in minutes","Buy out a whole concert or sneaker drop in the first second","Write a school essay from memory","Hold a long face-to-face conversation","Flood comment sections with fake posts faster than any moderator can read"]}]},{"h":"03: 2FA","read":"<p>Read <b>03: 2FA</b>, the three factors and the five examples ranked from weakest to strongest.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the 1.9 notes","href":"../unit-1/the-front-door.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q8","type":"match","q":"Match each factor to its example.","left":["Something you know","Something you have","Something you are"],"right":["A password or PIN","A fingerprint or your face","A phone or a security key"]},{"id":"q9","type":"order","q":"Put these 2FA examples in order from weakest to strongest, as the notes ranks them.","items":["Security key or fingerprint","Email verification code","SMS text code","Push notification approval","Authenticator app"]},{"id":"q10","type":"text","q":"What name does the notes give to the attack where someone tricks your carrier into moving your phone number onto their phone?","ph":"Two words"}]},{"h":"04: OWASP and the demo","read":"<p>Read <b>04: OWASP</b>. Then scroll to <b>Try it</b>, use the demo that changes the number at the end of the web address, and try it with the check on and off.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the 1.9 notes","href":"../unit-1/the-front-door.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q11","type":"mc","q":"What does OWASP stand for?","opts":["Official Website Authorization System","Open World Access Security Plan","Open Web Application Security Project","Online Web Attack Safety Program"]},{"id":"q12","type":"match","q":"Match each OWASP Top 10 item to its description.","left":["#1 Broken access control","#3 Injection"],"right":["A site never checks what you typed and obeys a trick phrase","A user can see or change things they were never allowed to touch"]},{"id":"q13","type":"text","q":"In the demo, you are student 1042. Change the number to 1043. Whose grades appear?","ph":"A first name"},{"id":"q14","type":"text","q":"In the demo, turn the check on and open /1043. What error code appears?","ph":"A three-digit code"},{"id":"q15","type":"write","q":"In your own words, explain why none of the four locks can cover for the other three. Use one example from the notes. (2 to 3 sentences)","min":25}]}]},"a":{"pts":100,"company":"Ticketmaster","color":"#026CDF","tagline":"You are a web-security intern. Your team is inspecting a ticket site before a big on-sale.","from":"Chris Vance, Web Security Lead","subject":"Inspect four test pages before the on-sale starts","email":"<p>Hi, tickets for a very popular show go on sale Friday. Before then I want you to inspect the site and tell me which of the four locks is missing, working, or being attacked.</p><p>The four locks are HTTPS, CAPTCHA, 2FA and OWASP. This is a classroom simulation, so the pages and findings are made up.</p>","steps":[{"h":"Inspect the pages","read":"<p>For each test page, pick the lock that is missing, working or under attack.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q1","type":"match","q":"Match each finding to the lock it is about.","left":["The checkout page uses http:// and asks for a credit card number","A script tries ten thousand password guesses in a second with no slowdown","A leaked password cannot log in because the account also asks for a phone code","A search box runs whatever commands visitors type into it"],"right":["HTTPS","2FA","OWASP (injection)","CAPTCHA"]},{"id":"q2","type":"mc","q":"A test page shows a padlock and https://, but the address is a near-perfect misspelling of a real ticket seller. What is the right finding?","opts":["The site is safe because of the padlock","CAPTCHA is missing on the page","The padlock is fake, so https:// is broken","HTTPS is present, but the site can still be fake"]}]},{"h":"On-sale day problems","read":"<p>Chris wants to know which lock handles each on-sale problem.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q3","type":"mc","q":"Bots buy out the first 5,000 tickets in two seconds. Which lock is meant to slow them down?","opts":["CAPTCHA, the doorman","HTTPS, the lock on the connection","2FA, the second deadbolt","OWASP, the building code"]},{"id":"q4","type":"order","q":"Rank these 2FA options for the team's fans from weakest to strongest.","items":["Push approval","Text message code","Security key","Authenticator app","Email code"]},{"id":"q5","type":"mc","q":"A fan changes the number at the end of an order address and sees a stranger's tickets. Which OWASP flaw is that?","opts":["Injection","Broken access control","A CAPTCHA failure","A missing certificate"]}]},{"h":"Write the summary","read":"<p>Chris needs a short summary for the launch meeting (three or four sentences).</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q6","type":"write","q":"Write the summary. Name the four locks, say what each one protects, and give one reason a fake site can still have a padlock.","min":45}]}]}},"2.1":{"id":"2.1","title":"Intro to Karel","back":"../unit-2/index.html","w":{"pts":50,"intro":"You will go through the Karel notes one part at a time, and write real Karel programs that run on the page. Each step names what to read, then asks questions or sets a Karel task. You need 100% to open the assignment, and you can check as many times as you like.","steps":[{"h":"Meet Karel","read":"<p>Open <b>Meet Karel</b> and read it all. Then open the <b>Intro to Karel</b> notes and read parts <b>00: What Is Karel?</b> and <b>01: Karel's Four Starter Commands</b>.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open Meet Karel","href":"../unit-2/meet-karel.html"},{"label":"Open the Intro to Karel notes","href":"../karel/intro-to-programming-with-karel.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q1","type":"mc","q":"What is Karel, according to Meet Karel?","opts":["A real robot that cleans classroom floors","A game character that plays all by itself","A virtual robot on a grid of streets and avenues","A website that grades your typing speed"]},{"id":"q2","type":"match","q":"Match each of Karel's four starter commands to what it does.","left":["move();","turnLeft();","putBall();","takeBall();"],"right":["Moves Karel forward one square in the direction it faces","Turns Karel 90 degrees to the left without moving","Picks up a ball from the corner Karel is standing on","Drops a ball on the corner Karel is standing on"]},{"id":"q3","type":"text","q":"What character ends almost every Karel command?","ph":"A symbol or its name"},{"id":"q4","type":"mc","q":"According to Meet Karel, what do the parentheses after a command like move() do?","opts":["They hold the number of squares to move","They tell Karel to turn after the move","They only make the code look neater","They run the command, which is called calling it"]},{"id":"q5","type":"mc","q":"The Intro notes say Karel has no built-in command for turning which way?","opts":["Right, so turnRight() does not exist yet","Left, so turnLeft() does not exist","Up, so Karel can never go north","Around, so Karel can never turn back"]}]},{"h":"Your first program","read":"<p>In the Intro notes find <b>Your first program</b> (the five lines read out as directions). Type it in the box below: forward two squares, turn left, forward one square, drop a ball. Press Run to watch Karel do it, then check your answer at the bottom.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the Intro to Karel notes","href":"../karel/intro-to-programming-with-karel.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q6","type":"karel","q":"Type the first program from the notes. Karel starts at avenue 1, street 1, facing East. Walk forward two squares, turn left, walk forward one more square, then drop a ball.","starter":"// your program goes here\n","world":{"cols":5,"rows":4,"x":1,"y":1,"dir":"E","balls":[],"walls":[]},"req":[],"kopts":{"superKarel":false,"allowLoose":true,"requireMain":false},"goal":{"x":3,"y":2,"dir":"N","balls":{"3,2":1}}}]},{"h":"Order matters","read":"<p>Read <b>02: Order Matters, A Lot</b> in the Intro notes.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the Intro to Karel notes","href":"../karel/intro-to-programming-with-karel.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q7","type":"mc","q":"Karel starts at avenue 1, street 1, facing East. After move(); turnLeft(); move(); which corner is Karel on?","opts":["Avenue 2, street 1","Avenue 2, street 2","Avenue 1, street 3","Avenue 3, street 1"]},{"id":"q8","type":"karel","q":"Make Karel end at avenue 3, street 2, facing East, with a ball on that corner. Karel starts at avenue 1, street 1, facing East. Remember: the order of the commands matters.","starter":"// your program goes here\n","world":{"cols":6,"rows":4,"x":1,"y":1,"dir":"E","balls":[],"walls":[]},"req":[],"kopts":{"superKarel":false,"allowLoose":true,"requireMain":false},"goal":{"x":4,"y":2,"dir":"E","balls":{"4,2":1}}}]},{"h":"Reading a world and longer programs","read":"<p>Open the <b>More Basic Karel</b> notes and read <b>00: Reading a Karel World</b>, <b>01: Combining Commands</b> and <b>02: Karel Can Only Carry So Much Information</b>.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the More Basic Karel notes","href":"../karel/more-basic-karel.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q9","type":"mc","q":"What does the notes say two turnLeft(); calls in a row do?","opts":["Turn Karel by only 45 degrees","Turn Karel to the right by 90 degrees","Turn Karel a full 180 degrees around","Leave Karel facing the same way as before"]},{"id":"q10","type":"mc","q":"What habit does the notes tell you to build before typing a single command?","opts":["Type the whole program and fix it afterward","Ask Karel to test the world on its own first","Copy a program from the last lesson exactly","Trace the path on the picture, counting every move"]},{"id":"q11","type":"karel","q":"Follow the longer example from the notes. Karel starts at avenue 1, street 1, facing East. Walk forward 3 squares, turn left, walk forward 1, drop a ball, walk forward 1 more, turn around, walk forward 1 back to the ball, and pick it up. Where does Karel end up?","starter":"// your program goes here\n","world":{"cols":6,"rows":5,"x":1,"y":1,"dir":"E","balls":[],"walls":[]},"req":[],"kopts":{"superKarel":false,"allowLoose":true,"requireMain":false},"goal":{"x":4,"y":2,"dir":"S","balls":{}}}]},{"h":"Common mistakes","read":"<p>Read <b>03: Common Mistakes</b> in both sets of notes.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the Intro to Karel notes","href":"../karel/intro-to-programming-with-karel.html"},{"label":"Open the More Basic Karel notes","href":"../karel/more-basic-karel.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q12","type":"mc","q":"What happens if you call takeBall(); on a corner with no ball?","opts":["The program crashes, like running into a wall","Karel moves on to the next corner without it","Karel quietly does nothing at all and goes on","Karel puts a ball down on that corner instead"]},{"id":"q13","type":"mc","q":"What does the notes advise about writing a long program?","opts":["Use only one turnLeft(); in a row","Test a few lines at a time","Write it all before running anything","Never test until it is perfect"]},{"id":"q14","type":"write","q":"In your own words, explain why Karel needs every instruction spelled out in order. Use one example from the notes. (2 to 3 sentences)","min":25}]}]},"a":{"pts":100,"company":"Amazon","color":"#FF9900","tagline":"You are a robotics intern at a fulfillment center. Your first job is to program a Karel-style robot to handle packages (the balls) with the four starter commands.","from":"Jordan Pierce, Robotics Floor Lead","subject":"Three package jobs for the new robot","email":"<p>Welcome to the floor. Our test robot understands four commands: move, turnLeft, putBall and takeBall. A package is a ball. The robot starts at avenue 1, street 1 facing East unless the map shows otherwise.</p><p>Write a program for each job. This is a classroom simulation, so the layout is made up.</p>","steps":[{"h":"Job 1: Drop a package","read":"<p>The robot must deliver one package to the loading dock.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q1","type":"karel","q":"Karel starts at avenue 1, street 1, facing East. Move 4 squares East and drop a package (ball) there. Karel should end at avenue 5, street 1, facing East.","starter":"// your program goes here\n","world":{"cols":6,"rows":4,"x":1,"y":1,"dir":"E","balls":[],"walls":[]},"req":[],"kopts":{"superKarel":false,"allowLoose":true,"requireMain":false},"goal":{"x":5,"y":1,"dir":"E","balls":{"5,1":1}}}]},{"h":"Job 2: Pick up and move a package","read":"<p>A package is waiting two squares North. Bring it to the corner two squares East of where the robot starts.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q2","type":"karel","q":"A package (ball) sits at avenue 1, street 3. Karel starts at avenue 1, street 1, facing East. Take the package and drop it at avenue 3, street 1. Karel can end facing any direction on avenue 3, street 1.","starter":"// your program goes here\n","world":{"cols":5,"rows":5,"x":1,"y":1,"dir":"E","balls":[[1,3,1]],"walls":[]},"req":[],"kopts":{"superKarel":false,"allowLoose":true,"requireMain":false},"goal":{"x":3,"y":1,"dir":"E","balls":{"3,1":1}}}]},{"h":"Job 3: Out and back","read":"<p>The robot must leave a package marker and return to its start, facing the way it began.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q3","type":"karel","q":"Karel starts at avenue 1, street 1, facing East. Move 3 squares East, drop a package (ball), then return to avenue 1, street 1 and face East again.","starter":"// your program goes here\n","world":{"cols":6,"rows":4,"x":1,"y":1,"dir":"E","balls":[],"walls":[]},"req":[],"kopts":{"superKarel":false,"allowLoose":true,"requireMain":false},"goal":{"x":1,"y":1,"dir":"E","balls":{"4,1":1}}}]},{"h":"Explain it to the team","read":"<p>Write two or three sentences for the training guide.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q4","type":"write","q":"Explain why the order of Karel's commands matters. Use one of your three jobs as the example.","min":30}]}]}},"2.3":{"id":"2.3","title":"Karel Can't Turn Right","back":"../unit-2/index.html","w":{"pts":50,"intro":"You will go through the Karel Can't Turn Right notes one part at a time, then write Karel programs that turn right using only turnLeft(). You need 100% to open the assignment, and you can check as many times as you like.","steps":[{"h":"Three lefts make a right","read":"<p>Open the notes and read the top of the page and <b>00: Wait... Karel Really Can't Turn Right?</b></p>","links":[{"label":"Open the Karel Can't Turn Right notes","href":"../karel/karel-cant-turn-right.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q1","type":"mc","q":"Which turning command does Karel know?","opts":["Only turnRight();","Neither; Karel can only move","Both turnLeft(); and turnRight();","Only turnLeft();"]},{"id":"q2","type":"mc","q":"Why does turning left three times end up facing the same way as turning right once?","opts":["Three quarter-turns left land where one right turn does","The third turnLeft(); is ignored by the program","Each turnLeft(); is secretly a right turn in disguise","Karel's turns always add up to exactly a full circle"]},{"id":"q3","type":"mc","q":"The notes call the three-line trick a mistake waiting to happen when it is typed over and over. What does the next lesson do about it?","opts":["Makes Karel turn automatically whenever it meets walls","Wraps the three lines in your own turnRight();","Adds a built-in turnRight(); to every single world","Removes the turnLeft(); command from Karel for good"]}]},{"h":"How many turnLeft() calls?","read":"<p>Read <b>01: How Many turnLeft(); Calls Do You Need?</b> and try the turn in real life as the notes suggest.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the Karel Can't Turn Right notes","href":"../karel/karel-cant-turn-right.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q4","type":"match","q":"Match what you want Karel to do with the number of turnLeft(); calls.","left":["Turn right","Turn around (face backward)","Keep facing the same way"],"right":["4 calls (or 0)","3 calls","2 calls"]},{"id":"q5","type":"mc","q":"A program uses two turnLeft(); calls hoping to turn right. Where does Karel end up facing relative to where it started?","opts":["The same way it faced at the start","To the right of where it was","Backward, the opposite way","To the left of where it was"]},{"id":"q6","type":"karel","q":"Turn right once. Karel starts at avenue 1, street 3, facing East. Move 2 squares East, turn right using only turnLeft(), move 2 squares, and drop a ball. Karel should end at avenue 3, street 1, facing South.","starter":"// your program goes here\n","world":{"cols":5,"rows":4,"x":1,"y":3,"dir":"E","balls":[],"walls":[]},"req":[],"kopts":{"superKarel":false,"allowLoose":true,"requireMain":false},"goal":{"x":3,"y":1,"dir":"S","balls":{"3,1":1}}},{"id":"q7","type":"karel","q":"Turn around. Karel starts at avenue 1, street 1, facing East. Move 3 squares East, turn around, and walk back to avenue 1. Karel should end at avenue 1, street 1, facing West.","starter":"// your program goes here\n","world":{"cols":5,"rows":3,"x":1,"y":1,"dir":"E","balls":[],"walls":[]},"req":[],"kopts":{"superKarel":false,"allowLoose":true,"requireMain":false},"goal":{"x":1,"y":1,"dir":"W","balls":{}}}]},{"h":"Walk a rectangle clockwise","read":"<p>Right turns let Karel walk clockwise. Use what you learned to walk a rectangle.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the Karel Can't Turn Right notes","href":"../karel/karel-cant-turn-right.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q8","type":"karel","q":"Karel starts at avenue 1, street 3, facing East. Walk clockwise around a rectangle: move 2 East, turn right, move 2 South, turn right, move 2 West, turn right, move 2 North, and turn right. Karel should end where it began, facing East.","starter":"// your program goes here\n","world":{"cols":5,"rows":4,"x":1,"y":3,"dir":"E","balls":[],"walls":[]},"req":[],"kopts":{"superKarel":false,"allowLoose":true,"requireMain":false},"goal":{"x":1,"y":3,"dir":"E","balls":{}}}]},{"h":"Common mistakes","read":"<p>Read <b>03: Common Mistakes</b>.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the Karel Can't Turn Right notes","href":"../karel/karel-cant-turn-right.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q9","type":"mc","q":"If Karel ends up facing the wrong way, what does the notes say is almost always the cause?","opts":["A wall in the world","A ball on the corner","A missing semicolon","A miscounted turnLeft();"]},{"id":"q10","type":"write","q":"In your own words, explain why the notes says this lesson is \"setting up the single biggest idea in the unit.\" (2 to 3 sentences)","min":25}]}]},"a":{"pts":100,"company":"iRobot","color":"#E31937","tagline":"You are a robotics intern working on a home-cleaning robot. The test robot can only turn left, like Karel, so you must write right turns yourself.","from":"Taylor Nguyen, Robot Navigation Team","subject":"Three navigation jobs for the test robot","email":"<p>Thanks for joining. Our test robot has only move, turnLeft, putBall and takeBall, so it cannot turn right directly. Each job below needs a right turn or a U-turn.</p><p>This is a classroom simulation, so the layouts are made up. The robot starts facing East unless the job says otherwise.</p>","steps":[{"h":"Job 1: Dock the robot","read":"<p>The robot starts upstairs and has to reach its charging dock, marked by a ball.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q1","type":"karel","q":"Karel starts at avenue 1, street 4, facing East. A ball (the charger) sits at avenue 4, street 2. Move to it, pick it up, and finish on that corner. Use only the four commands.","starter":"// your program goes here\n","world":{"cols":6,"rows":5,"x":1,"y":4,"dir":"E","balls":[[4,2,1]],"walls":[]},"req":[],"kopts":{"superKarel":false,"allowLoose":true,"requireMain":false},"goal":{"x":4,"y":2,"dir":"S","balls":{}}}]},{"h":"Job 2: Leave a marker and turn back","read":"<p>The robot leaves a marker ball and then goes back the way it came.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q2","type":"karel","q":"Karel starts at avenue 1, street 1, facing East. Move 4 squares, drop a ball, turn around, and move back to avenue 1. Karel should finish at avenue 1, street 1, facing West.","starter":"// your program goes here\n","world":{"cols":6,"rows":3,"x":1,"y":1,"dir":"E","balls":[],"walls":[]},"req":[],"kopts":{"superKarel":false,"allowLoose":true,"requireMain":false},"goal":{"x":1,"y":1,"dir":"W","balls":{"5,1":1}}}]},{"h":"Job 3: Circle a table clockwise","read":"<p>The robot must circle a table, keeping the table on its right, and drop a ball at each corner.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q3","type":"karel","q":"Karel starts at avenue 1, street 3, facing East. Walk clockwise around a rectangle: 2 squares East, turn right, 2 squares South, turn right, 2 squares West, turn right, 2 squares North, turn right. Drop a ball on each of the four corners as Karel reaches it (including the starting corner at the end). Karel should finish at the start facing East.","starter":"// your program goes here\n","world":{"cols":5,"rows":4,"x":1,"y":3,"dir":"E","balls":[],"walls":[]},"req":[],"kopts":{"superKarel":false,"allowLoose":true,"requireMain":false},"goal":{"x":1,"y":3,"dir":"E","balls":{"1,3":1,"3,3":1,"3,1":1,"1,1":1}}}]},{"h":"Explain the trick","read":"<p>Write a short note for the next intern.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q4","type":"write","q":"Explain how a robot that only turns left can turn right, and why that is annoying when it happens many times in a program. (2 to 3 sentences)","min":30}]}]}},"2.4":{"id":"2.4","title":"Functions in Karel","back":"../unit-2/index.html","w":{"pts":50,"intro":"You will go through the Functions in Karel notes and write functions that run on the page. You need 100% to open the assignment, and you can check as many times as you like.","steps":[{"h":"What is a function?","read":"<p>Open the notes and read the top of the page and <b>00: What Is a Function?</b></p>","links":[{"label":"Open the Functions in Karel notes","href":"../karel/functions-in-karel.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q1","type":"mc","q":"What is a function, according to the notes?","opts":["A named group of commands you define once and reuse","A comment that explains what the code does","A square somewhere in Karel's world","A command that Karel already knows how to do"]},{"id":"q2","type":"match","q":"Match each word with what it means in the notes.","left":["Defining a function","Calling a function","camelCase"],"right":["Using its name followed by () to run it","No spaces, each new word capitalized after the first, like turnRight","Describing a new command with the word function and curly braces"]},{"id":"q3","type":"text","q":"What word starts a function definition in the notes? Type just that word.","ph":"One word"}]},{"h":"Why bother with functions?","read":"<p>Read <b>02: Why Bother With Functions At All?</b></p>","links":[{"label":"Open the Functions in Karel notes","href":"../karel/functions-in-karel.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q4","type":"match","q":"Match each reason to its explanation.","left":["Less typing","Fewer mistakes","Easier to read"],"right":["If there is a bug you fix it in exactly one place","turnRight(); reads like plain English","Write the three turnLeft(); calls once and use the name a hundred times"]},{"id":"q5","type":"mc","q":"The notes compares a function to what in real life?","opts":["A broken tool that must be fixed by someone","A nickname for a whole routine, like \"get ready\"","A new wall that gets added to Karel's world","A map of the whole neighborhood around school"]}]},{"h":"Write your own functions","read":"<p>Now write functions yourself. The grader runs your whole program, so keep <code>function main()</code> at the bottom, as the notes show.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the Functions in Karel notes","href":"../karel/functions-in-karel.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q6","type":"karel","q":"Define a function called <code>turnRight</code> from three turnLeft(); calls. In main(), move 2 squares East, turn right, move 2 squares South and drop a ball. Karel starts at avenue 1, street 3, facing East.","starter":"// define turnRight here\n\nfunction main() {\n  // your program goes here\n}\n","world":{"cols":5,"rows":4,"x":1,"y":3,"dir":"E","balls":[],"walls":[]},"req":["Define a function called <code>turnRight</code>","Call <code>turnRight()</code>","Start your program in <code>function main()</code>"],"kopts":{"superKarel":false,"allowLoose":false,"requireMain":true},"goal":{"x":3,"y":1,"dir":"S","balls":{"3,1":1}}},{"id":"q7","type":"karel","q":"Define a function called <code>turnAround</code> that turns Karel around. In main(), move 3 squares East, turnAround, and move 3 squares back. Karel starts at avenue 1, street 1, facing East.","starter":"// define turnAround here\n\nfunction main() {\n  // your program goes here\n}\n","world":{"cols":5,"rows":3,"x":1,"y":1,"dir":"E","balls":[],"walls":[]},"req":["Define a function called <code>turnAround</code>","Call <code>turnAround()</code>","Start your program in <code>function main()</code>"],"kopts":{"superKarel":false,"allowLoose":false,"requireMain":true},"goal":{"x":1,"y":1,"dir":"W","balls":{}}}]},{"h":"Common mistakes","read":"<p>Read <b>03: Common Mistakes</b>.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the Functions in Karel notes","href":"../karel/functions-in-karel.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q8","type":"multi","q":"Which of these are the three common mistakes the notes lists?","opts":["Writing commands outside of any function","Using too many comments","Naming a function in camelCase","Forgetting a curly brace","Forgetting the parentheses"]},{"id":"q9","type":"mc","q":"If CodeHS says there is a problem but the line number looks fine, what does the notes say to check first?","opts":["Restart the browser","Rename the world","Count your curly braces","Delete the function"]},{"id":"q10","type":"write","q":"In your own words, give two reasons a programmer writes turnRight() as a function instead of typing three turnLeft(); calls each time. (2 to 3 sentences)","min":25}]}]},"a":{"pts":100,"company":"Domino's","color":"#006491","tagline":"You are a kitchen-robotics intern. The pizza-box robot can move and turn left only, so you will build useful commands as functions.","from":"Casey Brandt, Kitchen Automation","subject":"Build two reusable commands and use them","email":"<p>Hi, our box-stacking robot only knows move, turnLeft, putBall and takeBall. To make our programs short and readable we want reusable commands, written as functions. A box is a ball.</p><p>Always put your functions above main(). This is a classroom simulation, so the layouts are made up.</p>","steps":[{"h":"Job 1: A reusable right turn","read":"<p>Write turnRight() once and use it.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q1","type":"karel","q":"Karel starts at avenue 1, street 4, facing East. Define <code>turnRight</code>. In main(), move 3 East, turn right, move 3 South, turn right, move 2 West, and drop a ball. Karel ends at avenue 2, street 1, facing West.","starter":"// define turnRight here\n\nfunction main() {\n  // your program goes here\n}\n","world":{"cols":6,"rows":5,"x":1,"y":4,"dir":"E","balls":[],"walls":[]},"req":["Define a function called <code>turnRight</code>","Call <code>turnRight()</code>","Start your program in <code>function main()</code>"],"kopts":{"superKarel":false,"allowLoose":false,"requireMain":true},"goal":{"x":2,"y":1,"dir":"W","balls":{"2,1":1}}}]},{"h":"Job 2: Deliver and come back","read":"<p>Use a turnAround() function to deliver a box and come back.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q2","type":"karel","q":"Karel starts at avenue 1, street 1, facing East. Define <code>turnAround</code>. In main(): move 4 squares, drop a ball, turnAround, move 4 squares back. Karel finishes at avenue 1, street 1, facing West.","starter":"// define turnAround here\n\nfunction main() {\n  // your program goes here\n}\n","world":{"cols":6,"rows":3,"x":1,"y":1,"dir":"E","balls":[],"walls":[]},"req":["Define a function called <code>turnAround</code>","Call <code>turnAround()</code>","Start your program in <code>function main()</code>"],"kopts":{"superKarel":false,"allowLoose":false,"requireMain":true},"goal":{"x":1,"y":1,"dir":"W","balls":{"5,1":1}}}]},{"h":"Job 3: Use both","read":"<p>A box must travel East, South and back.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q3","type":"karel","q":"Karel starts at avenue 1, street 3, facing East. A box (ball) sits at avenue 3, street 3. Define <code>turnRight</code> and <code>turnAround</code>. In main(): move to the box and take it, turn right, move 2 South, drop the box, then turnAround and move 2 back North. Karel finishes at avenue 3, street 3, facing North.","starter":"// define turnRight and turnAround here\n\nfunction main() {\n  // your program goes here\n}\n","world":{"cols":5,"rows":4,"x":1,"y":3,"dir":"E","balls":[[3,3,1]],"walls":[]},"req":["Define a function called <code>turnRight</code>","Define a function called <code>turnAround</code>","Call <code>turnRight()</code>","Call <code>turnAround()</code>","Start your program in <code>function main()</code>"],"kopts":{"superKarel":false,"allowLoose":false,"requireMain":true},"goal":{"x":3,"y":3,"dir":"N","balls":{"3,1":1}}}]},{"h":"Explain why","read":"<p>Write a short note for the training guide.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q4","type":"write","q":"Explain two reasons the team wants reusable commands written as functions. Use turnRight() or turnAround() as an example. (2 to 3 sentences)","min":30}]}]}},"2.5":{"id":"2.5","title":"The Main Function","back":"../unit-2/index.html","w":{"pts":50,"intro":"You will go through the part of the Functions in Karel notes about main(), then write and repair programs that start in main(). You need 100% to open the assignment, and you can check as many times as you like.","steps":[{"h":"Where every program starts","read":"<p>Open the notes and read <b>01: The Main Function: Where Your Program Actually Starts</b>, the code example under it and the rule box.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the Functions in Karel notes","href":"../karel/functions-in-karel.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q1","type":"mc","q":"What does the notes compare main() to?","opts":["A comment about the program","The finish line of the program","A second copy of Karel","The start button of the program"]},{"id":"q2","type":"mc","q":"In the example, turnRight() is defined above main(). When does it actually run?","opts":["Only when main() calls it","Before main() starts","As soon as the file is read","Never; functions above main() are ignored"]},{"id":"q3","type":"mc","q":"What is the rule to remember about a command that is not inside a function main() eventually calls?","opts":["It runs once after main() finishes","It never runs, wherever it is written in the file","It runs once before main() ever starts","It runs only when Karel is on the first corner"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mc","q":"How does CodeHS read your file, according to the notes?","opts":["Bottom to top, starting with the very last line","Only the first ten lines and then it stops","Top to bottom, but it only starts acting at main()","Only the commands that are written inside comments"]}]},{"h":"Finish main()","read":"<p>These programs already define functions. Your job is to write main() so the right things happen in the right order.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the Functions in Karel notes","href":"../karel/functions-in-karel.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q5","type":"karel","q":"turnRight() is already written. Fill in main(): Karel starts at avenue 1, street 3, facing East. Move 1, turn right, move 1, then drop a ball. Karel finishes at avenue 2, street 2, facing South.","starter":"function turnRight() {\n  turnLeft();\n  turnLeft();\n  turnLeft();\n}\n\nfunction main() {\n  // write main here\n}\n","world":{"cols":4,"rows":4,"x":1,"y":3,"dir":"E","balls":[],"walls":[]},"req":["Call <code>turnRight()</code>","Start your program in <code>function main()</code>"],"kopts":{"superKarel":false,"allowLoose":false,"requireMain":true},"goal":{"x":2,"y":2,"dir":"S","balls":{"2,2":1}}},{"id":"q6","type":"karel","q":"A helper function <code>stepUp</code> is written for you. Fill in main() so Karel builds a three-step staircase by calling stepUp() three times. Karel starts at avenue 1, street 1, facing East. The goal picture shows a ball on avenue 1 street 1, avenue 2 street 2 and avenue 3 street 3, with Karel ending at avenue 4, street 4 facing East.","starter":"function stepUp() {\n  putBall();\n  move();\n  turnLeft();\n  move();\n  turnLeft();\n  turnLeft();\n  turnLeft();\n}\n\nfunction main() {\n  // call stepUp three times\n}\n","world":{"cols":5,"rows":5,"x":1,"y":1,"dir":"E","balls":[],"walls":[]},"req":["Call <code>stepUp()</code>","Start your program in <code>function main()</code>"],"kopts":{"superKarel":false,"allowLoose":false,"requireMain":true},"goal":{"x":4,"y":4,"dir":"E","balls":{"1,1":1,"2,2":1,"3,3":1}}}]},{"h":"Fix the broken program","read":"<p>Read <b>03: Common Mistakes</b> in the Functions notes, then fix the program below.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the Functions in Karel notes","href":"../karel/functions-in-karel.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q7","type":"karel","q":"This program has a command outside every function, so it does not run. Fix it so Karel moves 1 East, turns right, moves 1, and drops a ball. Karel starts at avenue 1, street 3, facing East and should finish at avenue 2, street 2, facing South with a ball there.","starter":"function turnRight() {\n  turnLeft();\n  turnLeft();\n  turnLeft();\n}\n\nmove();\n\nfunction main() {\n  turnRight();\n  move();\n  putBall();\n}\n","world":{"cols":4,"rows":4,"x":1,"y":3,"dir":"E","balls":[],"walls":[]},"req":["Call <code>turnRight()</code>","Start your program in <code>function main()</code>"],"kopts":{"superKarel":false,"allowLoose":false,"requireMain":true},"goal":{"x":2,"y":2,"dir":"S","balls":{"2,2":1}}},{"id":"q8","type":"mc","q":"In which function should a command live if you want Karel to run it?","opts":["Anywhere in the file, as long as it ends with a semicolon","Only at the top of the file","Only inside a comment","In main(), or in a function that main() calls"]},{"id":"q9","type":"write","q":"In your own words, explain why every program needs a main() function. (2 to 3 sentences)","min":25}]}]},"a":{"pts":100,"company":"FedEx","color":"#4D148C","tagline":"You are a logistics-software intern. The sorting robot's programs must start in main() and call small helper functions.","from":"Morgan Reese, Sorting Systems","subject":"Three programs for the sorting robot: all must start in main()","email":"<p>Hi, our sorting robot needs three small programs. All of them must start in <code>main()</code> and use helper functions. A package is a ball.</p><p>This is a classroom simulation, so the layouts are made up.</p>","steps":[{"h":"Job 1: Drop two packages","read":"<p>Two drop-off corners, two squares apart.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q1","type":"karel","q":"A helper function <code>dropOff</code> is already written: it drops a package and moves 2 squares. Write main() so Karel (avenue 1, street 1, facing East) calls dropOff three times. Karel ends at avenue 7, street 1, with a package on avenue 1, 3 and 5.","starter":"function dropOff() {\n  putBall();\n  move();\n  move();\n}\n\nfunction main() {\n  // write main here\n}\n","world":{"cols":8,"rows":3,"x":1,"y":1,"dir":"E","balls":[],"walls":[]},"req":["Call <code>dropOff()</code>","Start your program in <code>function main()</code>"],"kopts":{"superKarel":false,"allowLoose":false,"requireMain":true},"goal":{"x":7,"y":1,"dir":"E","balls":{"1,1":1,"3,1":1,"5,1":1}}}]},{"h":"Job 2: Corner delivery","read":"<p>A package must go around a corner using a right turn.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q2","type":"karel","q":"Karel starts at avenue 1, street 4, facing East, and there is a package at avenue 3, street 4. Write turnRight() yourself. In main(): move to the package, take it, turn right, move 3 squares, and drop it. Karel finishes at avenue 3, street 1, facing South.","starter":"// define turnRight here\n\nfunction main() {\n  // your program goes here\n}\n","world":{"cols":5,"rows":5,"x":1,"y":4,"dir":"E","balls":[[3,4,1]],"walls":[]},"req":["Define a function called <code>turnRight</code>","Call <code>turnRight()</code>","Start your program in <code>function main()</code>"],"kopts":{"superKarel":false,"allowLoose":false,"requireMain":true},"goal":{"x":3,"y":1,"dir":"S","balls":{"3,1":1}}}]},{"h":"Job 3: Repair this program","read":"<p>A teammate left a bug in the script.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q3","type":"karel","q":"This program does nothing useful because of where two commands are written. Fix it so Karel (avenue 1, street 3, facing East) moves 2 squares, turns right with turnRight(), and moves 2 squares. Karel should finish at avenue 3, street 1, facing South.","starter":"function turnRight() {\n  turnLeft();\n  turnLeft();\n  turnLeft();\n}\n\nmove();\nmove();\n\nfunction main() {\n  turnRight();\n  move();\n  move();\n}\n","world":{"cols":5,"rows":4,"x":1,"y":3,"dir":"E","balls":[],"walls":[]},"req":["Call <code>turnRight()</code>","Start your program in <code>function main()</code>"],"kopts":{"superKarel":false,"allowLoose":false,"requireMain":true},"goal":{"x":3,"y":1,"dir":"S","balls":{}}}]},{"h":"Explain why","read":"<p>Write a short note for the new hire.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q4","type":"write","q":"Explain what main() is for and why helper functions like dropOff() make main() easier to read. (2 to 3 sentences)","min":30}]}]}},"2.6":{"id":"2.6","title":"Top Down Design and Decomposition","back":"../unit-2/index.html","w":{"pts":50,"intro":"You will go through the Top Down Design notes and then plan and write bigger Karel programs by breaking them into functions. You need 100% to open the assignment, and you can check as many times as you like.","steps":[{"h":"Two big words","read":"<p>Open the notes and read the top of the page and <b>00: Two Big Words, One Simple Idea</b>.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the Top Down Design notes","href":"../karel/top-down-design-and-decomposition.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q1","type":"match","q":"Match each word to its meaning in the notes.","left":["Decomposition","Top down design"],"right":["Starting with the big goal and working down into smaller pieces, one layer at a time","Breaking a big task into smaller sub-tasks"]},{"id":"q2","type":"mc","q":"The notes breaks \"clean my room\" into four smaller jobs. Which is one of them?","opts":["Make the bed","Buy a new desk","Move to a new house","Paint the walls"]}]},{"h":"Decomposing a Karel task","read":"<p>Read <b>01: Decomposing a Karel Task</b> and look at both code examples.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the Top Down Design notes","href":"../karel/top-down-design-and-decomposition.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q3","type":"mc","q":"In the notes' bigger example (three towers in a row), what does main() look like?","opts":["An empty function that makes no calls at all","A short list of big steps: build, move, build","One giant loop that has no functions in it","A long list of every move and every putBall"]},{"id":"q4","type":"num","q":"How many balls does buildTower() drop in the notes?"},{"id":"q5","type":"num","q":"How many squares does moveToNextSpot() move Karel?"}]},{"h":"How to plan","read":"<p>Read <b>02: How to Actually Plan This Way</b> and <b>03: Common Mistakes</b>.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the Top Down Design notes","href":"../karel/top-down-design-and-decomposition.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q6","type":"order","q":"Put the four planning steps in the order the notes gives them.","items":["List the major steps in plain English","Call the functions in order inside main()","Turn each step into a function","Say the big goal in one sentence"]},{"id":"q7","type":"mc","q":"Why is a function named doStuff() a problem?","opts":["It would run twice","Functions cannot contain a capital letter","The name tells you nothing about what it does","It has too many letters"]}]},{"h":"Build it top down","read":"<p>Now do what the notes do: write the helper functions, then main().</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the Top Down Design notes","href":"../karel/top-down-design-and-decomposition.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q8","type":"karel","q":"Write one function, <code>buildTower</code>, that drops 4 balls on a corner, and call it from main(). Karel starts at avenue 1, street 1, facing East and should end there with a tower of 4 balls.","starter":"// write buildTower here\n\nfunction main() {\n  // call it here\n}\n","world":{"cols":5,"rows":3,"x":1,"y":1,"dir":"E","balls":[],"walls":[]},"req":["Define a function called <code>buildTower</code>","Call <code>buildTower()</code>","Start your program in <code>function main()</code>"],"kopts":{"superKarel":false,"allowLoose":false,"requireMain":true},"goal":{"x":1,"y":1,"dir":"E","balls":{"1,1":4}}},{"id":"q9","type":"karel","q":"Build 3 towers of 4 balls in a row, on avenues 1, 3 and 5 of street 1. Write <code>buildTower</code> and <code>moveToNextSpot</code> (two moves), and make main() read: build, move, build, move, build. Karel starts at avenue 1, street 1, facing East.","starter":"// write buildTower and moveToNextSpot here\n\nfunction main() {\n  // build, move, build, move, build\n}\n","world":{"cols":7,"rows":3,"x":1,"y":1,"dir":"E","balls":[],"walls":[]},"req":["Define a function called <code>buildTower</code>","Define a function called <code>moveToNextSpot</code>","Call <code>buildTower()</code>","Call <code>moveToNextSpot()</code>","Start your program in <code>function main()</code>"],"kopts":{"superKarel":false,"allowLoose":false,"requireMain":true},"goal":{"x":5,"y":1,"dir":"E","balls":{"1,1":4,"3,1":4,"5,1":4}}}]}]},"a":{"pts":100,"company":"LEGO","color":"#D01012","tagline":"You are a play-experience intern. Your team prototypes building robots that stack bricks, and each program must be planned top down.","from":"Riley Santos, Build Robotics","subject":"Plan it top down: three building programs","email":"<p>Hi, before anyone types a line of code, our rule is to say the goal, list the steps and turn each step into a function. A brick is a ball.</p><p>Write each program with clearly named helper functions and a short main(). This is a classroom simulation, so the layouts are made up.</p>","steps":[{"h":"Job 1: Two stacks","read":"<p>Two stacks of 3 bricks, two squares apart.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q1","type":"karel","q":"Karel starts at avenue 1, street 1, facing East. Build a stack of 3 bricks (balls) on avenue 1 and another stack of 3 on avenue 3. Write <code>buildStack</code> and <code>moveToNextSpot</code> (two moves), and keep main() short. Karel ends at avenue 3.","starter":"// write your helper functions here\n\nfunction main() {\n  // short and readable\n}\n","world":{"cols":6,"rows":3,"x":1,"y":1,"dir":"E","balls":[],"walls":[]},"req":["Define a function called <code>buildStack</code>","Define a function called <code>moveToNextSpot</code>","Call <code>buildStack()</code>","Call <code>moveToNextSpot()</code>","Use 24 lines of code or fewer","Start your program in <code>function main()</code>"],"kopts":{"superKarel":false,"allowLoose":false,"requireMain":true},"goal":{"x":3,"y":1,"dir":"E","balls":{"1,1":3,"3,1":3}}}]},{"h":"Job 2: A staircase wall","read":"<p>Each step needs a brick, a move and a climb.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q2","type":"karel","q":"Karel starts at avenue 1, street 1, facing East. Build a three-step staircase: put a brick on avenue 1, street 1, avenue 2, street 2 and avenue 3, street 3. Write <code>turnRight</code> and <code>placeAndClimb</code> (put a ball, move East, turn left, move North, turn right), and have main() call placeAndClimb three times. Karel ends at avenue 4, street 4 facing East.","starter":"// write your helper functions here\n\nfunction main() {\n  // call placeAndClimb three times\n}\n","world":{"cols":6,"rows":6,"x":1,"y":1,"dir":"E","balls":[],"walls":[]},"req":["Define a function called <code>turnRight</code>","Define a function called <code>placeAndClimb</code>","Call <code>placeAndClimb()</code>","Start your program in <code>function main()</code>"],"kopts":{"superKarel":false,"allowLoose":false,"requireMain":true},"goal":{"x":4,"y":4,"dir":"E","balls":{"1,1":1,"2,2":1,"3,3":1}}}]},{"h":"Job 3: Three towers","read":"<p>The full prototype from the training notes.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q3","type":"karel","q":"Karel starts at avenue 1, street 1, facing East. Build three towers of 4 bricks on avenues 1, 3 and 5. Use <code>buildTower</code> and <code>moveToNextSpot</code>, and write main() as build, move, build, move, build. It must also work in a wider world.","starter":"// write your helper functions here\n\nfunction main() {\n  // build, move, build, move, build\n}\n","world":{"cols":7,"rows":3,"x":1,"y":1,"dir":"E","balls":[],"walls":[]},"req":["Define a function called <code>buildTower</code>","Define a function called <code>moveToNextSpot</code>","Call <code>buildTower()</code>","Call <code>moveToNextSpot()</code>","Start your program in <code>function main()</code>","It must also work on 1 other world you cannot see (a different size or layout)"],"kopts":{"superKarel":false,"allowLoose":false,"requireMain":true},"goal":{"x":5,"y":1,"dir":"E","balls":{"1,1":4,"3,1":4,"5,1":4}}}]},{"h":"Explain your plan","read":"<p>Write a short note about how you planned.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q4","type":"write","q":"Explain, in the order of the four planning steps, how you would plan the three-tower program before writing code. (3 sentences)","min":35}]}]}},"2.7":{"id":"2.7","title":"Commenting Your Code and Abstraction","back":"../unit-2/index.html","w":{"pts":50,"intro":"You will go through the Commenting and Abstraction notes, then add comments and hide repeated code behind a function. You need 100% to open the assignment, and you can check as many times as you like.","steps":[{"h":"Comments","read":"<p>Open the Commenting notes and read all of it, parts 00 to 03.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the Commenting notes","href":"../karel/commenting-your-code.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q1","type":"text","q":"In CodeHS's Karel, which two characters start a comment on a line?","ph":"Type the two characters"},{"id":"q2","type":"mc","q":"What effect does a comment have on what Karel does?","opts":["It makes Karel move slower","It tells Karel to repeat the line","It turns the line into a function","None at all; Karel skips right over it"]},{"id":"q3","type":"match","q":"Match each kind of comment to where it goes.","left":["Comment on its own line","Comment at the end of a line"],"right":["A short note about just that one line of code","Usually right above the code it explains"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mc","q":"Which comment does the notes call genuinely useful?","opts":["// skip the 2nd corner, it has a ball","// move forward one square here","// this line is a comment about code","// call the move command now"]},{"id":"q5","type":"mc","q":"When should you write the comment for a function, according to the good habit box?","opts":["Days later, after you have finished testing","At the same time you write the function","Only at the moment when the program has a bug","Never, because Karel does not read them anyway"]},{"id":"q6","type":"karel","q":"Add a comment on the line right above each function in this program. Do not change what the program does: Karel (avenue 1, street 1, facing East) builds a tower of 3 balls, moves 2 squares, and builds another.","starter":"function buildTower() {\n  putBall();\n  putBall();\n  putBall();\n}\n\nfunction moveToNextSpot() {\n  move();\n  move();\n}\n\nfunction main() {\n  buildTower();\n  moveToNextSpot();\n  buildTower();\n}\n","world":{"cols":6,"rows":3,"x":1,"y":1,"dir":"E","balls":[],"walls":[]},"req":["Include at least 3 comments","Put a comment line above every function","Start your program in <code>function main()</code>"],"kopts":{"superKarel":false,"allowLoose":false,"requireMain":true},"goal":{"x":3,"y":1,"dir":"E","balls":{"1,1":3,"3,1":3}}}]},{"h":"Abstraction","read":"<p>Open the Abstraction notes and read all of it, parts 00 to 03.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the Abstraction notes","href":"../karel/abstraction.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q7","type":"mc","q":"What is abstraction, according to the notes?","opts":["Making every comment in the program longer","Deleting all of the code you do not need","Hiding messy details behind a simple name","Writing every single command in the code twice"]},{"id":"q8","type":"match","q":"Match each real-life example to what it hides.","left":["A car's gas pedal","A TV remote's power button","Ordering one burger"],"right":["The circuits that turn the TV on","How fuel injection and the engine work","Every step the kitchen takes to make it"]},{"id":"q9","type":"mc","q":"The notes say abstraction is NOT the same as deleting the detail. Why?","opts":["Because the detail is copied into a comment above it","Because the detail is stored on another computer far away","Because Karel remembers the detail forever and ever","The three turnLeft(); calls are still there, just hidden"]},{"id":"q10","type":"karel","q":"This program repeats the same three commands. Hide them behind one function called <code>dropAndStep</code> (put a ball, move, move) and call it three times in main(). Karel starts at avenue 1, street 1, facing East and drops a ball on avenues 1, 3 and 5.","starter":"function main() {\n  putBall();\n  move();\n  move();\n  putBall();\n  move();\n  move();\n  putBall();\n  move();\n  move();\n}\n","world":{"cols":8,"rows":3,"x":1,"y":1,"dir":"E","balls":[],"walls":[]},"req":["Define a function called <code>dropAndStep</code>","Call <code>dropAndStep()</code>","Start your program in <code>function main()</code>"],"kopts":{"superKarel":false,"allowLoose":false,"requireMain":true},"goal":{"x":7,"y":1,"dir":"E","balls":{"1,1":1,"3,1":1,"5,1":1}}},{"id":"q11","type":"write","q":"In your own words, explain how comments and abstraction help the next programmer who reads your code. (2 to 3 sentences)","min":25}]}]},"a":{"pts":100,"company":"GitHub","color":"#24292F","tagline":"You are an intern on a code-review team. Every submission must be readable: comments above functions and repeated code hidden behind a clear name.","from":"Dev Patel, Code Review Lead","subject":"Three review tasks: comments and abstraction","email":"<p>Hi, before code is merged we ask that it be easy to read. That means a comment above every function and no repeated blocks. Use our standard Karel robot for the tests.</p><p>This is a classroom simulation, so the layouts are made up.</p>","steps":[{"h":"Task 1: Add the missing comments","read":"<p>Reviewers rejected a submission with no comments.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q1","type":"karel","q":"Add a comment line above every function in the submission below (including main). The program must keep doing the same thing: Karel (avenue 1, street 3, facing East) moves 1 square, turns right, and moves 1 square. It ends at avenue 2, street 2, facing South.","starter":"function turnRight() {\n  turnLeft();\n  turnLeft();\n  turnLeft();\n}\n\nfunction main() {\n  move();\n  turnRight();\n  move();\n}\n","world":{"cols":5,"rows":4,"x":1,"y":3,"dir":"E","balls":[],"walls":[]},"req":["Put a comment line above every function","Start your program in <code>function main()</code>"],"kopts":{"superKarel":false,"allowLoose":false,"requireMain":true},"goal":{"x":2,"y":2,"dir":"S","balls":{}}}]},{"h":"Task 2: Hide the repetition","read":"<p>A submission repeats the same four commands. Abstract them.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q2","type":"karel","q":"The program below builds a staircase by repeating the same commands. Create a function <code>placeAndClimb</code> (put a ball, move, turn left, move, then turn right), call it three times from main(), and put a comment above every function. Karel starts at avenue 1, street 1, facing East and ends at avenue 4, street 4 facing East.","starter":"function turnRight() {\n  turnLeft();\n  turnLeft();\n  turnLeft();\n}\n\nfunction main() {\n  putBall();\n  move();\n  turnLeft();\n  move();\n  turnRight();\n  putBall();\n  move();\n  turnLeft();\n  move();\n  turnRight();\n  putBall();\n  move();\n  turnLeft();\n  move();\n  turnRight();\n}\n","world":{"cols":6,"rows":6,"x":1,"y":1,"dir":"E","balls":[],"walls":[]},"req":["Define a function called <code>turnRight</code>","Define a function called <code>placeAndClimb</code>","Call <code>placeAndClimb()</code>","Put a comment line above every function","Start your program in <code>function main()</code>"],"kopts":{"superKarel":false,"allowLoose":false,"requireMain":true},"goal":{"x":4,"y":4,"dir":"E","balls":{"1,1":1,"2,2":1,"3,3":1}}}]},{"h":"Task 3: Two towers, fully documented","read":"<p>Write it from scratch with comments and abstraction.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q3","type":"karel","q":"Karel starts at avenue 1, street 1, facing East. Build two towers of 3 balls, on avenue 1 and avenue 4. Write <code>buildTower</code> and <code>moveToNextSpot</code> (3 moves), a comment above each function, and keep main() to three lines.","starter":"// write your functions here\n\nfunction main() {\n}\n","world":{"cols":7,"rows":3,"x":1,"y":1,"dir":"E","balls":[],"walls":[]},"req":["Define a function called <code>buildTower</code>","Define a function called <code>moveToNextSpot</code>","Call <code>buildTower()</code>","Call <code>moveToNextSpot()</code>","Put a comment line above every function","Use 30 lines of code or fewer","Start your program in <code>function main()</code>"],"kopts":{"superKarel":false,"allowLoose":false,"requireMain":true},"goal":{"x":4,"y":1,"dir":"E","balls":{"1,1":3,"4,1":3}}}]},{"h":"Explain it","read":"<p>Write your review note.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q4","type":"write","q":"Explain, in your own words, the difference between what a comment does and what abstraction does. (2 to 3 sentences)","min":30}]}]}},"2.9":{"id":"2.9","title":"Super Karel","back":"../unit-2/index.html","w":{"pts":50,"intro":"You will go through the Super Karel notes and write programs that use the built-in turnRight() and turnAround(). You need 100% to open the assignment, and you can check as many times as you like.","steps":[{"h":"What is different about Super Karel?","read":"<p>Open the notes and read the top of the page and <b>00: What's Different About Super Karel?</b></p>","links":[{"label":"Open the Super Karel notes","href":"../karel/super-karel.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q1","type":"mc","q":"Which two commands does Super Karel add that regular Karel does not have?","opts":["turnRight(); and turnAround();","turnLeft(); and move();","putBall(); and takeBall();","jump(); and spin();"]},{"id":"q2","type":"mc","q":"What does the notes say about the two commands Super Karel adds?","opts":["They are brand new ideas you could not build yourself","They are two functions you already know how to build","They only work in the very last lesson of the unit","They replace the turnLeft(); command completely"]}]},{"h":"Same idea, less setup","read":"<p>Read <b>01: Same Idea, Less Setup</b> and <b>02: Why Learn to Build It First</b>.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the Super Karel notes","href":"../karel/super-karel.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q3","type":"mc","q":"Why does the notes say you learned to build turnRight() before getting Super Karel?","opts":["Because turnRight() is a very hard command to type","Because Super Karel did not even exist back then yet","So you would learn what a function is and build your own","Because the CodeHS company simply made a mistake"]},{"id":"q4","type":"karel","q":"Use Super Karel's built-in commands. Do NOT define turnRight() yourself. Karel starts at avenue 1, street 3, facing East: move 2, turn right, move 2, turn around, and move 2 back. Karel finishes at avenue 3, street 3, facing North.","starter":"function main() {\n  // use turnRight() and turnAround() directly\n}\n","world":{"cols":5,"rows":4,"x":1,"y":3,"dir":"E","balls":[],"walls":[]},"req":["Call <code>turnRight()</code>","Call <code>turnAround()</code>","Do not define <code>turnRight</code>: this Karel already has it","Do not define <code>turnAround</code>: this Karel already has it","Start your program in <code>function main()</code>"],"kopts":{"superKarel":true,"allowLoose":false,"requireMain":true},"goal":{"x":3,"y":3,"dir":"N","balls":{}}},{"id":"q5","type":"karel","q":"Build the staircase with Super Karel: three steps, each is put a ball, move, turn left, move, turn right. Do not define turnRight(). Karel starts at avenue 1, street 1, facing East and ends at avenue 4, street 4 facing East.","starter":"function main() {\n  // three steps\n}\n","world":{"cols":6,"rows":6,"x":1,"y":1,"dir":"E","balls":[],"walls":[]},"req":["Do not define <code>turnRight</code>: this Karel already has it","Start your program in <code>function main()</code>"],"kopts":{"superKarel":true,"allowLoose":false,"requireMain":true},"goal":{"x":4,"y":4,"dir":"E","balls":{"1,1":1,"2,2":1,"3,3":1}}}]},{"h":"Common mix-ups","read":"<p>Read <b>03: Common Mix-Ups</b>.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the Super Karel notes","href":"../karel/super-karel.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q6","type":"mc","q":"Super Karel adds turnRight(); and turnAround(). What does the notes say about other commands you might want?","opts":["They are never needed","Super Karel adds an unlimited set of commands","You must switch back to regular Karel","You still write your own function for them"]},{"id":"q7","type":"mc","q":"Even though turnRight() is built in, what is it still doing underneath?","opts":["The same three turnLeft(); calls","Nothing at all, since it is only a comment","A single secret turn that Karel hides","Four moves forward and then one turn back"]},{"id":"q8","type":"write","q":"In your own words, explain why Super Karel is a \"reasonable shortcut, not a mystery.\" (2 to 3 sentences)","min":25}]}]},"a":{"pts":100,"company":"Tesla","color":"#CC0000","tagline":"You are a factory-automation intern. The assembly robots are Super Karel: turnRight() and turnAround() are built in.","from":"Avery Kim, Assembly Line Software","subject":"Three assembly programs for the Super Karel robots","email":"<p>Hi, our assembly robots come with turnRight and turnAround built in, so please do not define them again. A part is a ball.</p><p>This is a classroom simulation, so the layouts are made up.</p>","steps":[{"h":"Job 1: Part transfer","read":"<p>Move a part from one station to another.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q1","type":"karel","q":"Karel starts at avenue 1, street 1, facing East. A part (ball) sits at avenue 4, street 1. Take it, turn around, bring it back and put it at avenue 1, street 1, facing West.","starter":"function main() {\n  \n}\n","world":{"cols":6,"rows":3,"x":1,"y":1,"dir":"E","balls":[[4,1,1]],"walls":[]},"req":["Call <code>turnAround()</code>","Do not define <code>turnAround</code>: this Karel already has it","Start your program in <code>function main()</code>"],"kopts":{"superKarel":true,"allowLoose":false,"requireMain":true},"goal":{"x":1,"y":1,"dir":"W","balls":{"1,1":1}}}]},{"h":"Job 2: Conveyor corner","read":"<p>A belt turns a corner twice.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q2","type":"karel","q":"Karel starts at avenue 1, street 4, facing East. Move 3 squares East, turn right, move 3 squares South, turn right, and move 3 squares West. Finish at avenue 1, street 1, facing West. Do not define turnRight().","starter":"function main() {\n  \n}\n","world":{"cols":6,"rows":5,"x":1,"y":4,"dir":"E","balls":[],"walls":[]},"req":["Call <code>turnRight()</code>","Do not define <code>turnRight</code>: this Karel already has it","Start your program in <code>function main()</code>"],"kopts":{"superKarel":true,"allowLoose":false,"requireMain":true},"goal":{"x":1,"y":1,"dir":"W","balls":{}}}]},{"h":"Job 3: Hurdle line","read":"<p>Parts must pass over a one-square-high hurdle on the belt.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q3","type":"karel","q":"Karel starts at avenue 1, street 1, facing East. There is a hurdle (a wall one square high) between avenue 2 and avenue 3. Move to the hurdle, then write a helper <code>jumpHurdle</code> that goes up, over and back down, and call it from main(), then move 1 more square. Do not define turnRight(). Karel should end at avenue 4, street 1, facing East.","starter":"// write jumpHurdle here\n\nfunction main() {\n  \n}\n","world":{"cols":6,"rows":4,"x":1,"y":1,"dir":"E","balls":[],"walls":[[2,1,"E"]]},"req":["Define a function called <code>jumpHurdle</code>","Call <code>jumpHurdle()</code>","Do not define <code>turnRight</code>: this Karel already has it","Start your program in <code>function main()</code>"],"kopts":{"superKarel":true,"allowLoose":false,"requireMain":true},"goal":{"x":4,"y":1,"dir":"E","balls":{}}}]},{"h":"Explain it","read":"<p>Write a note for the team.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q4","type":"write","q":"Explain what Super Karel gives you and why you still need to know how turnRight() works underneath. (2 to 3 sentences)","min":30}]}]}},"2.10":{"id":"2.10","title":"For Loops","back":"../unit-2/index.html","w":{"pts":50,"intro":"You will go through the For Loops notes and write loops that run on the page. You need 100% to open the assignment, and you can check as many times as you like.","steps":[{"h":"Why not copy and paste?","read":"<p>Open the notes and read the top of the page and <b>00: Why Not Just Copy-Paste?</b></p>","links":[{"label":"Open the For Loops notes","href":"../karel/for-loops.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q1","type":"mc","q":"What does a for loop do, according to the notes?","opts":["Repeats commands until a wall appears in front","Runs commands only when a ball is present","Repeats commands a fixed number of times","Defines a brand new command with a name"]},{"id":"q2","type":"mc","q":"If you need to change \"4\" to \"10\" later, how many places do you change in the loop version?","opts":["Ten","Four","None; it changes itself","Exactly one"]}]},{"h":"The three parts of the first line","read":"<p>Read <b>01: Breaking Down the Loop's First Line</b> and <b>02: Watch It Run</b>.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the For Loops notes","href":"../karel/for-loops.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q3","type":"match","q":"Match each part of <code>for (var i = 0; i &lt; 4; i++)</code> to what it means.","left":["var i = 0","i < 4","i++"],"right":["Keep looping as long as this is true","Start counting at 0","After each pass, add 1 to i"]},{"id":"q4","type":"num","q":"How many times does <code>for (var i = 0; i &lt; 4; i++)</code> run its body?"},{"id":"q5","type":"mc","q":"What is one trip through the loop's body called?","opts":["An iteration","A condition","A function","A comment"]}]},{"h":"Several commands in a loop","read":"<p>Read <b>03: Putting Multiple Commands Inside a Loop</b>.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the For Loops notes","href":"../karel/for-loops.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q6","type":"mc","q":"The loop <code>for (var i = 0; i &lt; 4; i++) { move(); putBall(); }</code> does what?","opts":["Drops 4 balls in a row, then moves 4 times","Move, put a ball, move, put a ball, four times","Moves 4 times in a row, then drops 4 balls","Moves just once and then drops just one ball"]},{"id":"q7","type":"karel","q":"Use a for loop that runs 4 times and does <code>move();</code> then <code>putBall();</code> each time. Karel starts at avenue 1, street 1, facing East. It ends at avenue 5 with a ball on avenues 2, 3, 4 and 5.","starter":"function main() {\n  // your loop goes here\n}\n","world":{"cols":7,"rows":3,"x":1,"y":1,"dir":"E","balls":[],"walls":[]},"req":["Use a for loop","Use 8 lines of code or fewer","Start your program in <code>function main()</code>","It must also work on 1 other world you cannot see (a different size or layout)"],"kopts":{"superKarel":false,"allowLoose":false,"requireMain":true},"goal":{"x":5,"y":1,"dir":"E","balls":{"2,1":1,"3,1":1,"4,1":1,"5,1":1}}},{"id":"q8","type":"karel","q":"Build a tower of 6 balls on one corner with a for loop. Karel starts at avenue 1, street 1, facing East and should end there with 6 balls on the corner.","starter":"function main() {\n  // your loop goes here\n}\n","world":{"cols":4,"rows":3,"x":1,"y":1,"dir":"E","balls":[],"walls":[]},"req":["Use a for loop","Use 6 lines of code or fewer","Start your program in <code>function main()</code>"],"kopts":{"superKarel":false,"allowLoose":false,"requireMain":true},"goal":{"x":1,"y":1,"dir":"E","balls":{"1,1":6}}}]},{"h":"Common mistakes","read":"<p>Read <b>04: Common Mistakes</b>.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the For Loops notes","href":"../karel/for-loops.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q9","type":"mc","q":"What happens if you write i <= 4 instead of i < 4 when starting from 0?","opts":["The loop runs only 3 times instead of 4","The loop never runs even one single time","The loop runs 5 times instead of 4","The loop runs forever and never stops"]},{"id":"q10","type":"mc","q":"What separates the three parts of a for loop's first line?","opts":["Spaces only","Commas, not semicolons","Colons","Semicolons, not commas"]},{"id":"q11","type":"mc","q":"If move(); would crash on its own, what happens inside a loop?","opts":["It crashes inside the loop too; loops do not check","Karel turns left to get around the wall","The loop skips that command safely and goes on","The loop moves Karel backward to avoid the wall"]}]}]},"a":{"pts":100,"company":"Minecraft","color":"#5B8731","tagline":"You are a world-builder intern. Your tools repeat the same block placements, so every build must use a for loop.","from":"Quinn Okoye, Build Tools Team","subject":"Three loop builds for the new builder bot","email":"<p>Hi, our builder bot places blocks (balls) while it walks. Every program below must use a for loop, so changing a number changes the build.</p><p>This is a classroom simulation, so the layouts are made up.</p>","steps":[{"h":"Build 1: A wall of blocks","read":"<p>Place a block on every square along a street.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q1","type":"karel","q":"Karel starts at avenue 1, street 1, facing East. Place a ball on avenue 1 and on every square it walks over for 6 more moves: use a for loop that runs 6 times with putBall(); then move();. Karel ends at avenue 7, street 1.","starter":"function main() {\n  \n}\n","world":{"cols":8,"rows":3,"x":1,"y":1,"dir":"E","balls":[],"walls":[]},"req":["Use a for loop","Use 8 lines of code or fewer","Start your program in <code>function main()</code>","It must also work on 1 other world you cannot see (a different size or layout)"],"kopts":{"superKarel":false,"allowLoose":false,"requireMain":true},"goal":{"x":7,"y":1,"dir":"E","balls":{"1,1":1,"2,1":1,"3,1":1,"4,1":1,"5,1":1,"6,1":1}}}]},{"h":"Build 2: Fence posts","read":"<p>A post every other square.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q2","type":"karel","q":"Karel starts at avenue 1, street 1, facing East. Use a for loop that runs 4 times: move 2 squares, then drop a ball. Karel ends at avenue 9, street 1 with a ball on avenues 3, 5, 7 and 9.","starter":"function main() {\n  \n}\n","world":{"cols":10,"rows":3,"x":1,"y":1,"dir":"E","balls":[],"walls":[]},"req":["Use a for loop","Use 9 lines of code or fewer","Start your program in <code>function main()</code>","It must also work on 1 other world you cannot see (a different size or layout)"],"kopts":{"superKarel":false,"allowLoose":false,"requireMain":true},"goal":{"x":9,"y":1,"dir":"E","balls":{"3,1":1,"5,1":1,"7,1":1,"9,1":1}}}]},{"h":"Build 3: A square frame","read":"<p>Mark the four corners of a square.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q3","type":"karel","q":"Karel starts at avenue 1, street 1, facing East. Walk a square with sides of 3 squares, counter-clockwise: use a for loop that runs 4 times, each time dropping a ball, moving 3 squares and turning left. Karel finishes where it started, facing East, with a ball on each of the four corners.","starter":"function main() {\n  \n}\n","world":{"cols":6,"rows":6,"x":1,"y":1,"dir":"E","balls":[],"walls":[]},"req":["Use a for loop","Use 12 lines of code or fewer","Start your program in <code>function main()</code>","It must also work on 1 other world you cannot see (a different size or layout)"],"kopts":{"superKarel":false,"allowLoose":false,"requireMain":true},"goal":{"x":1,"y":1,"dir":"E","balls":{"1,1":1,"4,1":1,"4,4":1,"1,4":1}}}]},{"h":"Explain it","read":"<p>Write a short note for the builders.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q4","type":"write","q":"Explain what the three parts of a for loop's first line do, using one of your builds as the example. (2 to 3 sentences)","min":30}]}]}},"2.11":{"id":"2.11","title":"If and If/Else Statements","back":"../unit-2/index.html","w":{"pts":50,"intro":"You will go through the If Statements and If/Else notes, then write programs that make decisions. Your programs are also run on a second world with different balls, so they must really decide. You need 100% to open the assignment, and you can check as many times as you like.","steps":[{"h":"Conditions","read":"<p>Open the If Statements notes and read the top of the page and <b>00: What Is a Condition?</b></p>","links":[{"label":"Open the If Statements notes","href":"../karel/if-statements-and-conditionals.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q1","type":"mc","q":"What is a condition, according to the notes?","opts":["A special kind of function with a name","A yes-or-no question: true or false","A command that moves Karel one square forward","A comment about what is in the world"]},{"id":"q2","type":"match","q":"Match each condition to the question it answers.","left":["frontIsClear()","frontIsBlocked()","ballsPresent()","noBallsPresent()"],"right":["Is there a ball on Karel's current corner?","Is Karel's current corner empty?","Is there NOT a wall directly in front of Karel?","Is there a wall directly in front of Karel?"]},{"id":"q3","type":"mc","q":"Does asking frontIsClear() move Karel?","opts":["Only if there is a ball","Yes, it moves Karel one square","No, it only checks","Yes, it turns Karel left"]}]},{"h":"The if statement","read":"<p>Read <b>01: The If Statement</b>, <b>02: A More Useful Example</b> and <b>03: Common Mistakes</b>.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the If Statements notes","href":"../karel/if-statements-and-conditionals.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q4","type":"mc","q":"If the front is blocked and you write if (frontIsClear()) { move(); }, what happens?","opts":["The program stops forever and never finishes","Karel crashes into the wall in front of it","Karel turns around and walks the other way","move(); is skipped and the program goes on"]},{"id":"q5","type":"mc","q":"What does the notes say about mixing up frontIsClear() and frontIsBlocked()?","opts":["They are opposites, so mixing them flips your logic","It does not matter which one you pick","They mean exactly the same thing in Karel","Only one of the two of them really exists"]},{"id":"q6","type":"karel","q":"Use a for loop that runs 5 times with an if statement inside: if there is a ball on the corner, take it; then move. Karel starts at avenue 1, street 1, facing East. This world has balls on some corners, and your program is also tested on another world.","starter":"function main() {\n  // for loop with an if inside\n}\n","world":{"cols":8,"rows":3,"x":1,"y":1,"dir":"E","balls":[[1,1,1],[3,1,1],[4,1,1],[7,1,1]],"walls":[]},"req":["Use a for loop","Use an if statement","Use 12 lines of code or fewer","Start your program in <code>function main()</code>","It must also work on 1 other world you cannot see (a different size or layout)"],"kopts":{"superKarel":false,"allowLoose":false,"requireMain":true},"goal":{"x":6,"y":1,"dir":"E","balls":{"7,1":1}}}]},{"h":"If / else","read":"<p>Open the If/Else notes and read all of it, parts 00 to 03.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the If/Else notes","href":"../karel/if-else-statements.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q7","type":"mc","q":"Read this: if (ballsPresent()) { takeBall(); } else { putBall(); } On a corner with no ball, what happens?","opts":["Karel takes a ball","Karel puts a ball down","Nothing happens","Karel crashes"]},{"id":"q8","type":"mc","q":"How many of the two blocks in an if/else run each time?","opts":["Both","It depends on the order","Exactly one","Neither"]},{"id":"q9","type":"mc","q":"What is wrong with else (ballsPresent()) { ... }?","opts":["It needs a semicolon at the end of the line","It must always come before the if statement","It must be written in capital letters only","else never takes a condition of its own"]},{"id":"q10","type":"karel","q":"Use a for loop that runs 6 times. On each corner, if there is a ball take it, else put one down; then move. Karel starts at avenue 1, street 1, facing East. It is also tested on another world.","starter":"function main() {\n  // for loop with if / else\n}\n","world":{"cols":8,"rows":3,"x":1,"y":1,"dir":"E","balls":[[2,1,1],[3,1,1]],"walls":[]},"req":["Use a for loop","Use an if statement","Use an else","Use 14 lines of code or fewer","Start your program in <code>function main()</code>","It must also work on 1 other world you cannot see (a different size or layout)"],"kopts":{"superKarel":false,"allowLoose":false,"requireMain":true},"goal":{"x":7,"y":1,"dir":"E","balls":{"1,1":1,"4,1":1,"5,1":1,"6,1":1}}}]},{"h":"In your own words","read":"<p>Read the last box of the If/Else notes.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the If/Else notes","href":"../karel/if-else-statements.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q11","type":"write","q":"In your own words, explain the difference between an if statement and an if/else statement. Use ballsPresent() in your example. (2 to 3 sentences)","min":25}]}]},"a":{"pts":100,"company":"Roomba","color":"#E31937","tagline":"You are a cleaning-robot intern. The robot decides what to do from what it finds on each square.","from":"Harper Lin, Cleaning Algorithms","subject":"Three decision programs for the cleaning robot","email":"<p>Hi, our robot walks along a hallway and has to react to what it finds: balls are crumbs, and an empty square may need a marker. Use if and if/else so the same program works on any hallway layout.</p><p>This is a classroom simulation. Your programs are tested on more than one layout.</p>","steps":[{"h":"Job 1: Clean the hallway","read":"<p>Pick up every crumb on the first 6 squares.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q1","type":"karel","q":"Karel starts at avenue 1, street 1, facing East. Use a for loop that runs 6 times: if there is a ball, take it; then move. All balls on avenues 1 to 6 must be gone and Karel ends on avenue 7.","starter":"function main() {\n  \n}\n","world":{"cols":9,"rows":3,"x":1,"y":1,"dir":"E","balls":[[1,1,1],[2,1,1],[5,1,1],[6,1,1]],"walls":[]},"req":["Use a for loop","Use an if statement","Use 12 lines of code or fewer","Start your program in <code>function main()</code>","It must also work on 2 other worlds you cannot see (a different size or layout)"],"kopts":{"superKarel":false,"allowLoose":false,"requireMain":true},"goal":{"x":7,"y":1,"dir":"E","balls":{}}}]},{"h":"Job 2: Mark the empty squares","read":"<p>Put a marker on every empty square.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q2","type":"karel","q":"Karel starts at avenue 1, street 1, facing East. Use a for loop that runs 7 times: if the corner has no ball, put one there; then move. Every corner on avenues 1 to 7 must end with exactly one ball.","starter":"function main() {\n  \n}\n","world":{"cols":9,"rows":3,"x":1,"y":1,"dir":"E","balls":[[2,1,1],[5,1,1]],"walls":[]},"req":["Use a for loop","Use an if statement","Use 12 lines of code or fewer","Start your program in <code>function main()</code>","It must also work on 1 other world you cannot see (a different size or layout)"],"kopts":{"superKarel":false,"allowLoose":false,"requireMain":true},"goal":{"x":8,"y":1,"dir":"E","balls":{"2,1":1,"5,1":1,"1,1":1,"3,1":1,"4,1":1,"6,1":1,"7,1":1}}}]},{"h":"Job 3: Flip every square","read":"<p>Swap each square: a crumb becomes clean, a clean square gets a marker.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q3","type":"karel","q":"Karel starts at avenue 1, street 1, facing East. Use a for loop that runs 8 times: if there is a ball take it, else put one down; then move. Every corner on avenues 1 to 8 must be flipped.","starter":"function main() {\n  \n}\n","world":{"cols":10,"rows":3,"x":1,"y":1,"dir":"E","balls":[[3,1,1],[4,1,1],[8,1,1]],"walls":[]},"req":["Use a for loop","Use an if statement","Use an else","Use 14 lines of code or fewer","Start your program in <code>function main()</code>","It must also work on 1 other world you cannot see (a different size or layout)"],"kopts":{"superKarel":false,"allowLoose":false,"requireMain":true},"goal":{"x":9,"y":1,"dir":"E","balls":{"1,1":1,"2,1":1,"5,1":1,"6,1":1,"7,1":1}}}]},{"h":"Explain it","read":"<p>Write a note for the team.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q4","type":"write","q":"Explain why Job 3 needs an if/else instead of two separate if statements. (2 to 3 sentences)","min":30}]}]}},"2.13":{"id":"2.13","title":"While Loops","back":"../unit-2/index.html","w":{"pts":50,"intro":"You will go through the While Loops notes, then write loops that work on streets of different lengths. Your programs are also run on worlds you cannot see. You need 100% to open the assignment, and you can check as many times as you like.","steps":[{"h":"When you don't know how many","read":"<p>Open the notes and read the top of the page and <b>00: When You Don't Know \"How Many\"</b>.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the While Loops notes","href":"../karel/while-loops.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q1","type":"mc","q":"When is a while loop the right tool?","opts":["When you do not know how many repeats you need","When you want to change Karel's direction","When you know the exact count","When you need to define a function"]},{"id":"q2","type":"mc","q":"Read this: while (frontIsClear()) { move(); } What does Karel do?","opts":["Turns left at every corner it passes","Keeps moving until it faces a wall","Moves exactly once and then stops moving","Moves exactly four times and then stops"]}]},{"h":"For loop or while loop?","read":"<p>Read <b>01: For Loop vs. While Loop</b> and <b>02: A Realistic Example</b>.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the While Loops notes","href":"../karel/while-loops.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q3","type":"match","q":"Match each loop to when it stops.","left":["for loop","while loop"],"right":["When the condition becomes false","When the counter reaches its limit"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mc","q":"Which rule of thumb does the notes give?","opts":["Always use a while loop for every repeat","Use whichever loop has the fewer letters","Known count: for loop. Depends on what Karel finds: while loop","Always use a for loop for every repeat"]},{"id":"q5","type":"karel","q":"Move Karel to the wall with a while loop. Karel starts at avenue 1, street 1, facing East and should end on the last avenue. It is tested on streets of different lengths, so do not count the squares.","starter":"function main() {\n  // your while loop goes here\n}\n","world":{"cols":6,"rows":3,"x":1,"y":1,"dir":"E","balls":[],"walls":[]},"req":["Use a while loop","Do not use a for loop","Use 7 lines of code or fewer","Start your program in <code>function main()</code>","It must also work on 3 other worlds you cannot see (a different size or layout)"],"kopts":{"superKarel":false,"allowLoose":false,"requireMain":true},"goal":{"x":6,"y":1,"dir":"E","balls":{}}}]},{"h":"Picking up along the way","read":"<p>Look again at the realistic example: it picks up balls until it hits the wall. Think about the last corner.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the While Loops notes","href":"../karel/while-loops.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q6","type":"karel","q":"Pick up every ball on the street. Karel starts at avenue 1, street 1, facing East and walks to the wall. In this world there is also a ball on the last corner, where Karel stops. Every ball must be gone, and Karel must finish on the last avenue.","starter":"function main() {\n  // while loop, then think about the last corner\n}\n","world":{"cols":7,"rows":3,"x":1,"y":1,"dir":"E","balls":[[1,1,1],[3,1,1],[7,1,1]],"walls":[]},"req":["Use a while loop","Use an if statement","Use 14 lines of code or fewer","Start your program in <code>function main()</code>","It must also work on 2 other worlds you cannot see (a different size or layout)"],"kopts":{"superKarel":false,"allowLoose":false,"requireMain":true},"goal":{"x":7,"y":1,"dir":"E","balls":{}}},{"id":"q7","type":"mc","q":"Why does the loop in the notes' realistic example leave a ball on the last corner?","opts":["Karel cannot pick up the last ball","The loop runs one extra time","takeBall(); does not work next to a wall","The loop ends when the front is blocked, before Karel checks the last corner"]}]},{"h":"Infinite loops","read":"<p>Read <b>03: The Danger: Infinite Loops</b>.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the While Loops notes","href":"../karel/while-loops.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q8","type":"mc","q":"What is an infinite loop?","opts":["A loop whose condition never becomes false","A loop that is written with no braces at all","A loop that runs exactly one single time","A for loop that uses a counter variable"]},{"id":"q9","type":"mc","q":"What must every while loop have inside it?","opts":["A turnAround(); call at the end of the loop","Something that eventually makes the condition false","A comment explaining what the loop does","A for loop nested inside of the while loop"]},{"id":"q10","type":"write","q":"In your own words, explain why this loop never ends: <code>while (frontIsClear()) { putBall(); }</code> and how to fix it. (2 to 3 sentences)","min":25}]}]},"a":{"pts":100,"company":"DoorDash","color":"#FF3008","tagline":"You are a delivery-routing intern. The delivery robot walks a street of unknown length, so every program must use a while loop.","from":"Sam Odell, Route Engineering","subject":"Three street programs that work on any street","email":"<p>Hi, our streets are different lengths and we do not know them ahead of time. Every program below must use a while loop and must work on streets you cannot see. A package is a ball.</p><p>This is a classroom simulation, so the layouts are made up.</p>","steps":[{"h":"Job 1: Drive to the end","read":"<p>The robot starts at the first house and must reach the last one.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q1","type":"karel","q":"Karel starts at avenue 1, street 2, facing East. Move to the wall using a while loop. It is tested on streets of different lengths.","starter":"function main() {\n  \n}\n","world":{"cols":7,"rows":3,"x":1,"y":2,"dir":"E","balls":[],"walls":[]},"req":["Use a while loop","Do not use a for loop","Use 7 lines of code or fewer","Start your program in <code>function main()</code>","It must also work on 2 other worlds you cannot see (a different size or layout)"],"kopts":{"superKarel":false,"allowLoose":false,"requireMain":true},"goal":{"x":7,"y":2,"dir":"E","balls":{}}}]},{"h":"Job 2: Leave a package at every house","read":"<p>Every house gets one package, including the last one.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q2","type":"karel","q":"Karel starts at avenue 1, street 1, facing East. Drop one ball on every corner of the street, including the last corner, then stop at the wall. It is tested on streets of different lengths.","starter":"function main() {\n  \n}\n","world":{"cols":6,"rows":3,"x":1,"y":1,"dir":"E","balls":[],"walls":[]},"req":["Use a while loop","Use 9 lines of code or fewer","Start your program in <code>function main()</code>","It must also work on 2 other worlds you cannot see (a different size or layout)"],"kopts":{"superKarel":false,"allowLoose":false,"requireMain":true},"goal":{"x":6,"y":1,"dir":"E","balls":{"1,1":1,"2,1":1,"3,1":1,"4,1":1,"5,1":1,"6,1":1}}}]},{"h":"Job 3: Collect the returns","read":"<p>Some houses have a return package waiting.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q3","type":"karel","q":"Karel starts at avenue 1, street 1, facing East. Walk to the wall and take every ball on the way, including a ball on the first and the last corner. It is tested on layouts you cannot see.","starter":"function main() {\n  \n}\n","world":{"cols":8,"rows":3,"x":1,"y":1,"dir":"E","balls":[[1,1,1],[4,1,2],[8,1,1]],"walls":[]},"req":["Use a while loop","Use 14 lines of code or fewer","Start your program in <code>function main()</code>","It must also work on 2 other worlds you cannot see (a different size or layout)"],"kopts":{"superKarel":false,"allowLoose":false,"requireMain":true},"goal":{"x":8,"y":1,"dir":"E","balls":{}}}]},{"h":"Explain it","read":"<p>Write a short note for the team.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q4","type":"write","q":"Explain why these programs use a while loop instead of a for loop, and what happens at the last corner. (2 to 3 sentences)","min":30}]}]}},"2.14":{"id":"2.14","title":"Debugging Strategies","back":"../unit-2/index.html","w":{"pts":50,"intro":"You will go through the Debugging Strategies notes, then fix three broken programs the way the notes says: say what should happen, find the exact line, and change one thing. You need 100% to open the assignment, and you can check as many times as you like.","steps":[{"h":"The debugging process","read":"<p>Open the notes and read the top of the page and <b>00: The Debugging Process</b>.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the Debugging Strategies notes","href":"../karel/debugging-strategies.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q1","type":"order","q":"Put the four debugging steps in the order the notes gives them.","items":["Find the exact line","Say what SHOULD happen","Say what ACTUALLY happens","Change ONE thing"]},{"id":"q2","type":"mc","q":"What does the notes call the number-one mistake beginners make?","opts":["Writing far too many comments in the code","Using a for loop where a while loop fits","Randomly changing code and re-running it","Naming a function too clearly for its job"]},{"id":"q3","type":"mc","q":"Why should you change only one thing at a time?","opts":["Karel can only read one single line at a time","CodeHS only allows one change for each run","It always makes the finished code much shorter","You will not know which of the changes fixed it"]}]},{"h":"Two kinds of bugs and four techniques","read":"<p>Read <b>01: Two Kinds of Bugs</b> and <b>02: Techniques That Actually Help</b>.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the Debugging Strategies notes","href":"../karel/debugging-strategies.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q4","type":"match","q":"Match each kind of bug to what it looks like.","left":["Crash","Wrong result, no crash"],"right":["The program finishes but Karel ends in the wrong place or the balls are wrong","Karel runs into a wall or takes a ball that is not there, and the program stops"]},{"id":"q5","type":"multi","q":"Which of these are techniques the notes lists? Pick all that apply.","opts":["Trace it on paper first","Re-read the world before you code","Check your braces and parentheses","Run it one function at a time","Delete the whole program and start over","Add more turnLeft(); calls until it works"]}]},{"h":"Fix the bugs","read":"<p>Each program below has one bug. Say what should happen, run it, find the line, and change one thing.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the Debugging Strategies notes","href":"../karel/debugging-strategies.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q6","type":"karel","q":"Bug 1 (a crash). This program should move Karel 4 squares East and drop a ball. Karel starts at avenue 1, street 1, facing East in a world 5 squares wide. Fix the one bug so it ends on avenue 5 with a ball.","starter":"function main() {\n  for (var i = 0; i <= 4; i++) {\n    move();\n  }\n  putBall();\n}\n","world":{"cols":5,"rows":3,"x":1,"y":1,"dir":"E","balls":[],"walls":[]},"req":["Use a for loop","Start your program in <code>function main()</code>"],"kopts":{"superKarel":false,"allowLoose":false,"requireMain":true},"goal":{"x":5,"y":1,"dir":"E","balls":{"5,1":1}}},{"id":"q7","type":"karel","q":"Bug 2 (wrong result, no crash). Karel starts at avenue 1, street 3, facing East. The program should move 2 East, turn right, and move 2 South so that Karel ends at avenue 3, street 1 facing South. It ends in the wrong place. Fix it.","starter":"function turnRight() {\n  turnLeft();\n  turnLeft();\n}\n\nfunction main() {\n  move();\n  move();\n  turnRight();\n  move();\n  move();\n}\n","world":{"cols":5,"rows":4,"x":1,"y":3,"dir":"E","balls":[],"walls":[]},"req":["Define a function called <code>turnRight</code>","Start your program in <code>function main()</code>"],"kopts":{"superKarel":false,"allowLoose":false,"requireMain":true},"goal":{"x":3,"y":1,"dir":"S","balls":{}}},{"id":"q8","type":"karel","q":"Bug 3 (a program that never ends). This should drop one ball on every corner from where Karel stands up to the wall (but not on the last corner). Karel starts at avenue 1, street 1, facing East. It is tested on several street lengths. Fix it.","starter":"function main() {\n  while (frontIsClear()) {\n    putBall();\n  }\n}\n","world":{"cols":6,"rows":3,"x":1,"y":1,"dir":"E","balls":[],"walls":[]},"req":["Use a while loop","Start your program in <code>function main()</code>","It must also work on 2 other worlds you cannot see (a different size or layout)"],"kopts":{"superKarel":false,"allowLoose":false,"requireMain":true},"goal":{"x":6,"y":1,"dir":"E","balls":{"1,1":1,"2,1":1,"3,1":1,"4,1":1,"5,1":1}}}]},{"h":"Say it in words","read":"<p>Think about how you found each bug.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the Debugging Strategies notes","href":"../karel/debugging-strategies.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q9","type":"write","q":"Describe how you would debug a program where Karel finishes in the wrong place but nothing crashes. Use the steps from the notes. (3 sentences)","min":30}]}]},"a":{"pts":100,"company":"Netflix","color":"#E50914","tagline":"You are a quality-assurance intern. Test programs for the robot camera dolly came back broken, and you find and fix the bugs.","from":"Jamie Alvarez, QA Engineering","subject":"Three buggy robot programs to fix","email":"<p>Hi, three programs for the camera robot failed testing. For each one, say what should happen, find the exact line, and change one thing. A prop is a ball.</p><p>This is a classroom simulation, so the layouts are made up. Programs are tested on more than one layout.</p>","steps":[{"h":"Bug A: The robot hits the wall","read":"<p>The dolly must slide 5 squares and place a prop, but it crashes.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q1","type":"karel","q":"Karel starts at avenue 1, street 1, facing East in a world 6 squares wide. It should slide 5 squares East and drop a prop. The program crashes. Fix the one bug.","starter":"function main() {\n  for (var i = 0; i <= 5; i++) {\n    move();\n  }\n  putBall();\n}\n","world":{"cols":6,"rows":3,"x":1,"y":1,"dir":"E","balls":[],"walls":[]},"req":["Use a for loop","Start your program in <code>function main()</code>","It must also work on 1 other world you cannot see (a different size or layout)"],"kopts":{"superKarel":false,"allowLoose":false,"requireMain":true},"goal":{"x":6,"y":1,"dir":"E","balls":{"6,1":1}}}]},{"h":"Bug B: The robot faces the wrong way","read":"<p>The dolly finishes pointing the wrong way.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q2","type":"karel","q":"Karel starts at avenue 1, street 1, facing East. It should move 3 squares, turn around, and move back 3 squares, finishing on avenue 1 facing West. It finishes facing the wrong way. Fix the bug.","starter":"function turnAround() {\n  turnLeft();\n  turnLeft();\n  turnLeft();\n}\n\nfunction main() {\n  move();\n  move();\n  move();\n  turnAround();\n  move();\n  move();\n  move();\n}\n","world":{"cols":5,"rows":3,"x":1,"y":1,"dir":"E","balls":[],"walls":[]},"req":["Define a function called <code>turnAround</code>","Start your program in <code>function main()</code>"],"kopts":{"superKarel":false,"allowLoose":false,"requireMain":true},"goal":{"x":1,"y":1,"dir":"W","balls":{}}}]},{"h":"Bug C: The robot never stops","read":"<p>The dolly keeps going and never finishes.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q3","type":"karel","q":"Karel starts at avenue 1, street 1, facing East. It should pick up every ball along the street and stop at the wall, including a ball on the last corner. The program never ends. Fix it, so it also works on other streets.","starter":"function main() {\n  while (frontIsClear()) {\n    if (ballsPresent()) {\n      takeBall();\n    }\n  }\n}\n","world":{"cols":7,"rows":3,"x":1,"y":1,"dir":"E","balls":[[2,1,1],[7,1,1]],"walls":[]},"req":["Use a while loop","Start your program in <code>function main()</code>","It must also work on 2 other worlds you cannot see (a different size or layout)"],"kopts":{"superKarel":false,"allowLoose":false,"requireMain":true},"goal":{"x":7,"y":1,"dir":"E","balls":{}}}]},{"h":"Write the bug report","read":"<p>Write a short report for the QA board.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q4","type":"write","q":"Pick one of the three bugs and write the report: what should happen, what actually happened, which line was wrong, and the one change you made. (3 to 4 sentences)","min":40}]}]}},"2.15":{"id":"2.15","title":"How to Indent Your Code","back":"../unit-2/index.html","w":{"pts":50,"intro":"You will go through the How to Indent Your Code notes and then re-indent messy programs. The grader checks that your program still works AND that every line is indented the way the notes says. You need 100% to open the assignment, and you can check as many times as you like.","steps":[{"h":"Indentation is for humans","read":"<p>Open the notes and read the top of the page, <b>00: An Important Difference From Some Languages</b> and <b>01: Same Code, Two Ways</b>.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the How to Indent notes","href":"../karel/how-to-indent-your-code.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q1","type":"mc","q":"In this version of Karel, what controls which commands belong to which function or loop?","opts":["The curly braces { }, not the spacing","The indentation of each of the lines","The line numbers shown on the left side","The comments written beside the code"]},{"id":"q2","type":"mc","q":"If indentation does not change what the program does, why bother?","opts":["Karel runs noticeably faster with indenting","Unindented code is nearly impossible for people to follow","Indenting is required by CodeHS to score the code","The program will not run at all without it"]}]},{"h":"The rule","read":"<p>Read <b>02: The Rule</b>, including the table of nesting levels.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the How to Indent notes","href":"../karel/how-to-indent-your-code.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q3","type":"mc","q":"What should a closing brace } do?","opts":["Sit at the far right","Go on the same line as the next command","Line up with the line that opened it","Be indented one extra level"]},{"id":"q4","type":"match","q":"Match each nesting level to what is typically there.","left":["Level 0 (no indent)","Level 1 (indented once)","Level 2 (indented twice)","Level 3 (indented three times)"],"right":["function main() { and its closing }","Commands directly inside main, such as a for loop's first line","Commands inside that if statement","Commands inside that for loop, such as an if statement's first line"]},{"id":"q5","type":"karel","q":"Re-indent this program using 2 spaces for each level. Do not change any command. Karel starts at avenue 1, street 1, facing East and ends at avenue 5 with a ball on avenues 2, 3, 4 and 5.","starter":"function main() {\nfor (var i = 0; i < 4; i++) {\nmove();\nputBall();\n}\n}\n","world":{"cols":7,"rows":3,"x":1,"y":1,"dir":"E","balls":[],"walls":[]},"req":["Use a for loop","Indent your code correctly (2 or 4 spaces per level)","Start your program in <code>function main()</code>"],"kopts":{"superKarel":false,"allowLoose":false,"requireMain":true},"goal":{"x":5,"y":1,"dir":"E","balls":{"2,1":1,"3,1":1,"4,1":1,"5,1":1}}},{"id":"q6","type":"karel","q":"Re-indent this program with an if statement inside a for loop. Use 2 spaces per level. Karel starts at avenue 1, street 1, facing East. It takes a ball from any corner that has one, for 5 squares.","starter":"function main() {\nfor (var i = 0; i < 5; i++) {\nif (ballsPresent()) {\ntakeBall();\n}\nmove();\n}\n}\n","world":{"cols":7,"rows":3,"x":1,"y":1,"dir":"E","balls":[[2,1,1],[4,1,1]],"walls":[]},"req":["Use a for loop","Use an if statement","Indent your code correctly (2 or 4 spaces per level)","Start your program in <code>function main()</code>"],"kopts":{"superKarel":false,"allowLoose":false,"requireMain":true},"goal":{"x":6,"y":1,"dir":"E","balls":{}}}]},{"h":"Quick check","read":"<p>Read the quick check box and the last line of the notes.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the How to Indent notes","href":"../karel/how-to-indent-your-code.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q7","type":"mc","q":"What is the quick check the notes gives?","opts":["If there are no comments, then the indentation is fine","If the program runs, then the indentation is fine","If every line starts in column 1, then it is fine","If you cannot match each } to its { by indentation, it is not consistent yet"]},{"id":"q8","type":"write","q":"In your own words, explain why indentation is \"for humans, not the computer.\" (2 to 3 sentences)","min":25}]}]},"a":{"pts":100,"company":"Slack","color":"#4A154B","tagline":"You are a developer-experience intern. Your team's style guide says every program must be indented consistently before it is shared.","from":"Ari Whitfield, Developer Experience","subject":"Clean up three programs before the review","email":"<p>Hi, three programs were written with no indentation. Please re-indent them using two spaces for each level and do not change what they do. Closing braces line up with the line that opened them.</p><p>This is a classroom simulation, so the layouts are made up. The grader checks both the behavior and the indentation.</p>","steps":[{"h":"Program 1: Diagonal","read":"<p>Karel climbs a diagonal and drops a ball on each step.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q1","type":"karel","q":"Re-indent this program (2 spaces per level). Karel starts at avenue 1, street 1, facing East.","starter":"function turnRight() {\nturnLeft();\nturnLeft();\nturnLeft();\n}\n\nfunction main() {\nfor (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) {\nputBall();\nmove();\nturnLeft();\nmove();\nturnRight();\n}\n}\n","world":{"cols":6,"rows":6,"x":1,"y":1,"dir":"E","balls":[],"walls":[]},"req":["Define a function called <code>turnRight</code>","Indent your code correctly (2 or 4 spaces per level)","Start your program in <code>function main()</code>"],"kopts":{"superKarel":false,"allowLoose":false,"requireMain":true},"goal":{"x":4,"y":4,"dir":"E","balls":{"1,1":1,"2,2":1,"3,3":1}}}]},{"h":"Program 2: Pick up along the way","read":"<p>A while loop with an if inside it.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q2","type":"karel","q":"Re-indent this program (2 spaces per level). Karel starts at avenue 1, street 1, facing East and walks to the wall taking balls.","starter":"function main() {\nwhile (frontIsClear()) {\nif (ballsPresent()) {\ntakeBall();\n}\nmove();\n}\nif (ballsPresent()) {\ntakeBall();\n}\n}\n","world":{"cols":7,"rows":3,"x":1,"y":1,"dir":"E","balls":[[1,1,1],[4,1,1],[7,1,1]],"walls":[]},"req":["Use a while loop","Use an if statement","Indent your code correctly (2 or 4 spaces per level)","Start your program in <code>function main()</code>","It must also work on 1 other world you cannot see (a different size or layout)"],"kopts":{"superKarel":false,"allowLoose":false,"requireMain":true},"goal":{"x":7,"y":1,"dir":"E","balls":{}}}]},{"h":"Program 3: if/else in a loop","read":"<p>An else block adds another level to track.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q3","type":"karel","q":"Re-indent this program (2 spaces per level). Pay attention to the line <code>} else {</code>, which closes one block and opens another. Karel starts at avenue 1, street 1, facing East.","starter":"function main() {\nfor (var i = 0; i < 5; i++) {\nif (ballsPresent()) {\ntakeBall();\n} else {\nputBall();\n}\nmove();\n}\n}\n","world":{"cols":7,"rows":3,"x":1,"y":1,"dir":"E","balls":[[2,1,1],[3,1,1]],"walls":[]},"req":["Use a for loop","Use an if statement","Use an else","Indent your code correctly (2 or 4 spaces per level)","Start your program in <code>function main()</code>","It must also work on 1 other world you cannot see (a different size or layout)"],"kopts":{"superKarel":false,"allowLoose":false,"requireMain":true},"goal":{"x":6,"y":1,"dir":"E","balls":{"1,1":1,"4,1":1,"5,1":1}}}]},{"h":"Explain it","read":"<p>Write a note for the style guide.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q4","type":"write","q":"Write the style-guide rule for indenting Karel code in your own words, with one example of what is at each level. (2 to 3 sentences)","min":30}]}]}},"2.16":{"id":"2.16","title":"Control Structures","back":"../unit-2/index.html","w":{"pts":50,"intro":"You will go through the Control Structures notes, a review that combines functions, loops and conditions, and then write programs that use all of them. Your programs are also tested on worlds you cannot see. You need 100% to open the assignment, and you can check as many times as you like.","steps":[{"h":"The goal in plain English","read":"<p>Open the notes and read the top of the page, <b>00: The Goal</b> and <b>01: Breaking It Into Pieces</b>.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the Control Structures notes","href":"../karel/control-structures-example.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q1","type":"match","q":"Match each plain-English step to the control structure the notes uses for it.","left":["Keep going until the wall","At each corner, check for a ball","Turn around at the end"],"right":["Your own turnAround() function (or Super Karel's)","An if / else checking ballsPresent()","A while loop checking frontIsClear()"]},{"id":"q2","type":"mc","q":"The notes say the goal is written in plain English before any code. Which habit from an earlier lesson is that?","opts":["Top-down design","Indenting","Debugging","Commenting your code"]}]},{"h":"The full program","read":"<p>Read <b>02: The Full Program</b> and <b>03: Every Control Structure From This Unit</b>.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the Control Structures notes","href":"../karel/control-structures-example.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q3","type":"mc","q":"In the full program, what does the while loop control?","opts":["Which way Karel is facing as it goes along","How long the work repeats, until a wall","How many balls Karel is carrying in its bag","Which action happens each time through the loop"]},{"id":"q4","type":"mc","q":"In the full program, what does the if / else control?","opts":["Whether Karel turns left at the end of it","Which function main() calls at the very start","Which action happens each time: take or put","How long the loop keeps on running for"]},{"id":"q5","type":"karel","q":"Build the notes' program, but make it correct on the last corner too. Karel starts at avenue 1, street 1, facing East at one end of a street of unknown length. At every corner including the last: if there is a ball take it, else put one down. Then turn around at the wall. Define turnAround yourself.","starter":"// write turnAround here\n\nfunction main() {\n  // while loop with if / else, then the last corner, then turn around\n}\n","world":{"cols":6,"rows":3,"x":1,"y":1,"dir":"E","balls":[[2,1,1],[3,1,1],[6,1,1]],"walls":[]},"req":["Define a function called <code>turnAround</code>","Call <code>turnAround()</code>","Use a while loop","Use an if statement","Use an else","Start your program in <code>function main()</code>","It must also work on 2 other worlds you cannot see (a different size or layout)"],"kopts":{"superKarel":false,"allowLoose":false,"requireMain":true},"goal":{"x":6,"y":1,"dir":"W","balls":{"1,1":1,"4,1":1,"5,1":1}}}]},{"h":"More Karel examples","read":"<p>Hurdles are a classic Karel problem. A hurdle is a wall one square high. Karel goes up, over and down, and you do not know where the hurdles are in advance.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the Control Structures notes","href":"../karel/control-structures-example.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q6","type":"karel","q":"Random Hurdles. Karel starts at avenue 1, street 1, facing East in a world 8 squares wide with hurdles in different places. Use a for loop that runs 7 times: if the front is clear move, else jump the hurdle. Write <code>turnRight</code> and <code>jumpHurdle</code> yourself. Karel finishes at avenue 8, street 1.","starter":"// write turnRight and jumpHurdle here\n\nfunction main() {\n  // for loop with if / else\n}\n","world":{"cols":8,"rows":4,"x":1,"y":1,"dir":"E","balls":[],"walls":[[2,1,"E"],[5,1,"E"]]},"req":["Define a function called <code>turnRight</code>","Define a function called <code>jumpHurdle</code>","Call <code>jumpHurdle()</code>","Use a for loop","Use an if statement","Use an else","Start your program in <code>function main()</code>","It must also work on 3 other worlds you cannot see (a different size or layout)"],"kopts":{"superKarel":false,"allowLoose":false,"requireMain":true},"goal":{"x":8,"y":1,"dir":"E","balls":{}}}]},{"h":"Choosing the tool","read":"<p>Read the last paragraphs of the notes.</p>","links":[{"label":"Open the Control Structures notes","href":"../karel/control-structures-example.html"}],"items":[{"id":"q7","type":"match","q":"Match each job to the best tool.","left":["Repeat exactly 7 times","Repeat until Karel reaches a wall","Do something only when a ball is here","Choose between two actions each time","Give a group of commands a reusable name"],"right":["An if / else statement","A function","An if statement","A while loop","A for loop"]},{"id":"q8","type":"write","q":"In your own words, explain how a while loop and an if/else work together in the full program. (2 to 3 sentences)","min":25}]}]},"a":{"pts":100,"company":"Waymo","color":"#00A0B0","tagline":"You are a self-driving software intern. The cleanup robot at the test track must handle streets and obstacles it has never seen.","from":"Robin Castillo, Test Track Software","subject":"Three programs that must work on layouts we have not shown you","email":"<p>Hi, the test track changes every day. Your programs must use functions, loops and conditions together and must work on layouts you cannot see. A cone is a ball.</p><p>This is a classroom simulation, so the layouts are made up.</p>","steps":[{"h":"Job 1: Cleanup Karel","read":"<p>Remove every cone from the street, whatever its length.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q1","type":"karel","q":"Karel starts at avenue 1, street 1, facing East. Take every ball on the street (at most one per corner) including the first and last corners, walking to the wall. It is tested on other streets.","starter":"function main() {\n  \n}\n","world":{"cols":7,"rows":3,"x":1,"y":1,"dir":"E","balls":[[1,1,1],[3,1,1],[7,1,1]],"walls":[]},"req":["Use a while loop","Use an if statement","Start your program in <code>function main()</code>","It must also work on 2 other worlds you cannot see (a different size or layout)"],"kopts":{"superKarel":false,"allowLoose":false,"requireMain":true},"goal":{"x":7,"y":1,"dir":"E","balls":{}}}]},{"h":"Job 2: Hurdle course","read":"<p>Random hurdles on the track.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q2","type":"karel","q":"Karel starts at avenue 1, street 1, facing East in a world 9 squares wide, with hurdles in places you cannot see. Cross the whole street with a for loop that runs 8 times: if the front is clear move, else jump the hurdle. Write <code>turnRight</code> and <code>jumpHurdle</code> and finish at avenue 9.","starter":"// write your functions here\n\nfunction main() {\n  \n}\n","world":{"cols":9,"rows":4,"x":1,"y":1,"dir":"E","balls":[],"walls":[[3,1,"E"],[6,1,"E"]]},"req":["Define a function called <code>turnRight</code>","Define a function called <code>jumpHurdle</code>","Call <code>jumpHurdle()</code>","Use a for loop","Use an if statement","Use an else","Start your program in <code>function main()</code>","It must also work on 3 other worlds you cannot see (a different size or layout)"],"kopts":{"superKarel":false,"allowLoose":false,"requireMain":true},"goal":{"x":9,"y":1,"dir":"E","balls":{}}}]},{"h":"Job 3: Fill and return","read":"<p>Fill the street, then come back.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q3","type":"karel","q":"Karel starts at avenue 1, street 1, facing East. Put exactly one ball on every corner of the street, but only where there is none already (including the last corner). Then turn around and walk back to avenue 1. Write <code>turnAround</code> yourself. It is tested on other streets.","starter":"// write turnAround here\n\nfunction main() {\n  \n}\n","world":{"cols":6,"rows":3,"x":1,"y":1,"dir":"E","balls":[[2,1,1],[6,1,1]],"walls":[]},"req":["Define a function called <code>turnAround</code>","Call <code>turnAround()</code>","Use a while loop","Use an if statement","Start your program in <code>function main()</code>","It must also work on 2 other worlds you cannot see (a different size or layout)"],"kopts":{"superKarel":false,"allowLoose":false,"requireMain":true},"goal":{"x":1,"y":1,"dir":"W","balls":{"2,1":1,"6,1":1,"1,1":1,"3,1":1,"4,1":1,"5,1":1}}}]},{"h":"Explain your choices","read":"<p>Write a note for the track team.</p>","links":[],"items":[{"id":"q4","type":"write","q":"Explain which control structure you used for each part of Job 3 (filling, checking each corner, turning around, walking back) and why. (3 to 4 sentences)","min":40}]}]}}}};
